// Kiddo School — Class 37 · Sports, Movement & Play: Learning Path lessons.
// One individual teaching lesson per sport (owner doctrine: Learning Path =
// HOW to learn). Each lesson opens on its own /learn/{slug}/ page: the
// original Canva flashcard on the left, this step-by-step teaching content on
// the right. Content is written fresh for teaching — the library card pages
// in data-sports.mjs answer WHAT the sport is and HOW to try it at home, and
// no text is shared between the two. The teaching vocabulary follows the
// early-years language of the US (gross motor skills), Canada (physical
// literacy) and Australia & New Zealand (fundamental movement skills — run,
// jump, throw, catch, kick, balance, strike). The "notice" field describes
// only the card's own template facts: the sport word at the top and the
// sport's own scene below — nothing invented about the artwork.
export const lpContent={
 soccer:{
  title:'The Soccer Lesson',one:'The kicking game of the whole world — how feet learn to stop a ball, steer a ball and love a ball.',
  seoTitle:'Soccer Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Kicking, Dribbling and Stopping (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the soccer kick, the plant foot, the freeze-stop and the first dribble, with a living-room goal game that builds gross motor skills.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches soccer the way four-year-olds actually meet it: foot meets ball, ball goes somewhere, and the somewhere gets steered — one gentle step at a time.',
  what:'Soccer is a team game played with one ball and mostly feet: kick it to a friend, dribble it down the field, and kick it into the net to score. Only the goalkeepers may use hands.',
  mean:'For a small child soccer is the first sport where a tool is replaced by a body part: the foot must do what a hand would do — aim, carry, stop. That switch-on is why soccer is the world\u2019s first sport for four-year-olds in almost every country.',
  know:'Your child needs three soccer ideas: the kick is a swing from the hip with the toe down; the OTHER foot plants beside the ball like a anchor; and the freeze-stop — one sole on top of the ball — is how players own the ball instead of chasing it.',
  why:'Soccer opens this class because it is the most universal gross motor activity on the wall: a ball, a patch of ground, done. It is also the friendliest miss in sport — a ball that rolls away simply waits to be kicked again.',
  dev:'Kicking (the last of the big ball skills to mature), single-leg balance on the plant foot, steering a rolling object with taps — the dribble — plus turn-taking in the goal game.',
  vocab:'soccer, kick, goal, dribble, stop, field, team',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is soccer — the game where feet do everything."\n2) Demo the kick slowly: plant foot beside the ball, swing the kicking leg from the hip, toe down, contact. Make the plant foot the star — "this foot stands still like a tree."\n3) Teach the freeze-stop: roll the ball gently, your child runs it down and puts one sole on top — freeze! Practise three rolls.\n4) First dribble: kick, walk, kick, walk — the ball stays a pet on a leash, never running away.\n5) Finish with the chair-goal game below and the takeaway sentence: "I kick, I stop, the ball is mine."',
  say:'"Soccer is kicking the ball with your feet — hands stay home! Plant your strong foot next to the ball like a tree, swing the other one, toe down… and there it goes. And when the ball tries to run away? You put your foot on top and say FREEZE. The ball stops. The ball is yours."',
  notice:'On this card the word "soccer" sits in big letters at the top, with the game\u2019s own scene below — the sport exactly as your child will meet it on the field, one ball, big grass.',
  ask:'"What do we kick the ball with in soccer?" "Can we pick it up?" "How do we stop the ball when it runs away?" "Where does a goal go in?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "You kick it with your feet!" "No hands!" or the proud one: "I froze the ball — it stopped!"',
  real:'Any grass, any hallway, any ball. Point out soccer everywhere it lives: the park game on Sunday, the World Cup on the screen, the neighbour\u2019s kid dribbling past the fence. One real "I kick like that too!" is worth ten drills.',
  play:'Freeze-the-Ball: you roll the ball gently across the room, your child chases it down and freezes it with one foot on top. Then swap — you freeze, they roll. Three freezes each, then the chair-goal opens and every freeze earns one shot. Roll, chase, freeze, shoot — the whole first soccer class, played.',
  tips:'If the kick keeps missing, shrink the ball, not the child: a size-1 mini ball or even a soft toy is easier to steer and builds the same swing. And never rush the hands rule — goalkeepers get hands, and today your child can BE the goalkeeper for one round, diving on the ball with both hands like the pros.',
  check:'Ask: "Which foot plants like a tree? What do we say when the foot lands on the ball? How many hands does a goalkeeper get?" Plant foot, freeze, two — three answers and the first lesson of the class is won.'},
 basketball:{
  title:'The Basketball Lesson',one:'Bounce, catch, drop it in the hoop — the up-down game that teaches small hands to talk to a ball.',
  seoTitle:'Basketball Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Bouncing, Catching and Hoop Shots (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the basketball bounce, the soft catch and the drop-in shot, with a laundry-basket hoop game for hand-eye coordination.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches basketball from the palm down: the ball lives on the floor, comes back up by itself, and every bounce is a conversation between hand and ball.',
  what:'Basketball is a team game with a bouncing ball and two raised hoops: dribble by bouncing, pass by throwing, score by dropping the ball through the hoop.',
  mean:'Basketball is the only major sport where the ball asks to come back — bounce it and it returns to your hand. For a child that return is irresistible: it makes ten repetitions feel like ten turns of a game, which is exactly how throwing skills get their reps.',
  know:'Your child needs three basketball ideas: the bounce is a PUSH, not a slap — fingers spread, ball pressed down; the catch is soft hands that pull the ball in like a hug; and a shot is an arc — up and over, not at the hoop.',
  why:'Of every card on this wall, basketball grows hand-eye coordination fastest, because bounce-and-catch repeats in seconds. It also teaches the vertical game — up, over, down — that volleyball and tennis will borrow later.',
  dev:'Object control (push-catch), hand-eye timing, arc awareness for aiming at raised targets, and the two-hand pattern that underpins every catch in sport.',
  vocab:'basketball, bounce, dribble, catch, hoop, arc, shot',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is basketball — the bouncing game."\n2) Hand out the bounce: fingers spread like a starfish, push the ball down at your side. It comes back — that is the magic. Five pushes.\n3) Teach the soft catch: arms out, fingers wide, pull the ball IN as it arrives — hugging hands, not fence posts. Ten gentle tosses.\n4) Show the arc: shots go UP and over, like a rainbow into the basket. Fire straight and the rim says no.\n5) Play the basket game below and close with: "push down, catch soft, shoot rainbows."',
  say:'"Basketball is the bouncing game! Push the ball down with your starfish hand — and look, it comes back up to say hello. Catch it soft, like a hug. And when we shoot, we shoot a rainbow — up, up, over, and… swish!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "basketball" stands big at the top with the game\u2019s scene underneath — the bouncing ball and the hoop exactly as your child will meet them on court.',
  ask:'"What does the ball do when we push it down?" "Do we catch it hard or soft?" "Does a shot go straight, or up like a rainbow?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It comes back by itself!" "I caught it soft!" or the shot report: "My rainbow went in — swish!"',
  real:'Hoops are everywhere once you look: the park court, the school yard, the mini hoop on a bedroom door. Watch a minute of a real game and find the rainbow shots together — arcs, arcs everywhere.',
  play:'Laundry-Basket Hoop: basket on the floor first — bounce five, then drop-toss it in. Round two: basket on a chair — now the rainbow matters. Round three: basket moves, shooter moves. Every make is a swish-shout, every miss is "one more rainbow." The basket never laughs at a miss, and neither do we.',
  tips:'If the bounce keeps escaping, make the hand quieter: push, do not slap, and start the bounce at knee height, not shoulder. And let the ball be silly sometimes — one bounce on the HEAD is worth ten drills for keeping the fun alive.',
  check:'Ask: "Push or slap? Catch hard or soft? Straight or rainbow?" Push, soft, rainbow — three answers and the ball talks to your child now.'},
 volleyball:{
  title:'The Volleyball Lesson',one:'The no-drop game — bumps that keep the ball alive in the air and a team that talks.',
  seoTitle:'Volleyball Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Bump, Keep-It-Up and Team Talk (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the volleyball bump, the keep-it-up rally and the count-the-hits team game, with a balloon drill that never touches the floor.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches volleyball\u2019s one big rule first — the ball never touches the floor — and lets the bump grow out of saving it.',
  what:'Volleyball is a net game between two teams: bump, set or spike the ball over, and never let it land on your side. Three touches is the grown-up limit; one glorious bump is the four-year-old limit.',
  mean:'Volleyball is the only sport on this wall where the ground is the enemy. That single rule makes children lift their eyes off the floor and into the air — a whole new ball-sport posture, and the reason volleyball players read the sky.',
  know:'Your child needs three volleyball ideas: the bump is FLAT forearms joined like a shelf, not hands; the ball is met, not hit — legs push up as the ball lands on the shelf; and the team shout "mine!" is part of the skill.',
  why:'The bump is the gentlest contact skill in sport — no grip, no swing, just a shelf that helps the ball up. It is the perfect first team sport for children who find hitting a ball hard but love keeping things alive.',
  dev:'Underarm contact control, reading a falling object, the joined-forearm platform, and calling "mine!" — the first team-communication skill on the whole wall.',
  vocab:'volleyball, bump, net, rally, keep it up, mine, team',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is volleyball — the no-drop game."\n2) Build the shelf: arms straight down, forearms together, flat as a table. Ball rests on the shelf — hold it there three seconds.\n3) Teach the meet: when the ball comes down, do not swing — legs push, shelf lifts, ball floats up. Like bouncing it off a trampoline you wear.\n4) Count together: one bump, catch, two bumps, catch — bumps grow one at a time.\n5) Play balloon-volleyball below and end on the team word: "mine!" before every touch.',
  say:'"Volleyball is the no-drop game! We make a shelf with our arms — flat and strong — and when the ball floats down, our legs lift it back up. Bump! We never let it touch the floor. And we always shout MINE before we bump — that is how teams talk."',
  notice:'On this card the word "volleyball" is printed big at the top, the game\u2019s scene below it — the floating ball over the net exactly as the sport looks from the sand or the court.',
  ask:'"What do we never let the ball touch?" "Do we hit it with fists or meet it with a flat shelf?" "What do we shout before we bump?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The floor is out!" "I made a shelf!" or the proud count: "Three bumps — no drops!"',
  real:'Volleyball lives at beaches, backyards and school gyms — and its cousin lives in every living room: balloons. Watch a rally point together and hunt for the bump: flat arms, ball up, team shouting.',
  play:'Balloon Keep-It-Up: a balloon over a string or sofa-cushion net. One bump at a time, catch between rounds, count every bump out loud. When singles get steady, join hands-family-style and bump as a two-person team — "MINE!" before every touch. The balloon hangs long enough for the shelf to find it, and the no-drop rule does the coaching.',
  tips:'If hands keep sneaking up, tape a soft bracelet on the wrists as a reminder: the bump lives below the bracelet. And let the balloon win sometimes — a chaotic, giggling, everyone-diving rally is the sport\u2019s best advertisement.',
  check:'Ask: "What must never touch the floor? What do our arms make? What do we shout?" Floor, a shelf, MINE — three answers and the rally is on.'},
 tennis:{
  title:'The Tennis Lesson',one:'The rally game — a racket, a floating ball and the back-and-forth that never wants to end.',
  seoTitle:'Tennis Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Racket Taps, Tracking and Rallies (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the tennis grip, the sideways tap and the first rally, with balloon-tap games that build racket skills and fast eyes.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches tennis as one delicious trick: keep the ball in the air with a thing on the end of your arm — the rally grows out of that trick like a plant.',
  what:'Tennis is a net game played with rackets: hit the ball over the net, your partner hits it back, and the rally keeps going as long as nobody misses.',
  mean:'Tennis is the first sport on the wall where the hand plays through a tool. The racket adds reach but subtracts direct touch — the brain must remap hitting, and once it does, every striking sport gets easier.',
  know:'Your child needs three tennis ideas: the racket face is a flat palm held out in front; the ball is TAPPED, not smashed — a high-five through the ball; and rallies are counting games, not winning games.',
  why:'Tennis earns an early card because striking is one of the big fundamental movement skills and the balloon-first version needs no court, no net and no strength — just a floating thing worth chasing.',
  dev:'Striking with an implement, tracking a moving object through the air, side-on body position, and the patience of a rally that counts instead of competes.',
  vocab:'tennis, racket, tap, net, rally, over, count',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is tennis — the tap-it-over game."\n2) Make the palm-racket connection: hold your flat hand up — that IS the racket face. Now the racket holds the same palm for you.\n3) Tap-up drill: drop the balloon, tap it up with the flat face, let it fall, tap again. Count the taps together — every record is a party.\n4) Turn sideways: feet point at the wall, belly not the net — the sideways stand of every real rally.\n5) Play sofa-net rallies below and close: "flat palm, little tap, over it goes."',
  say:'"Tennis is the tap-it-over game! Your racket is a flat palm on a stick — tap the balloon UP, let it float, tap again. How many taps can we do? Then we turn our body side-on like a real player and tap it over the sofa-net to me. Ping! My turn. Ping! Your turn."',
  notice:'On this card the word "tennis" leads at the top with the game\u2019s scene under it — the racket, the ball and the net exactly as the rally looks from the court.',
  ask:'"What does the racket face look like?" "Do we smash or tap?" "What do we count in a rally?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "My racket is a big flat hand!" "I tapped it seven times!" or the rally shout: "Ping — your turn!"',
  real:'Tennis lives at parks, clubs and TVs — and its tiny cousins live in every kitchen: a spoon and a pom-pom, a paddle and a balloon. Watch one rally together and hunt for the tap.',
  play:'Sofa-Net Rally: the sofa back is the net, the balloon is the ball. Stand two cushions apart, tap it over, partner taps it back. Balloon rallies last long enough to feel REAL — and when a rally reaches five, the whole room loses its mind. That is the moment tennis hooks a child for good.',
  tips:'If the racket scares the game away, play hand-tennis first — the tap is the same skill without the tool. And pick the lightest racket sold: junior foam rackets are toys that happen to be real.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the racket face? Tap or smash? What does a rally count?" Flat palm, tap, taps — three answers and the rally has begun.'},
 badminton:{
  title:'The Badminton Lesson',one:'The floating game — a feathered shuttle that hangs in the air long enough for everyone to catch up.',
  seoTitle:'Badminton Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Shuttle Tracking and Gentle Taps (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the badminton grip, the underarm tap and shuttle-watching, with floating-shuttle games for slow, steady rallies.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches badminton around its kindest gift: the shuttlecock floats, drifts and waits — the slowest ball in sport, and the best teacher of watching eyes.',
  what:'Badminton is a net game with rackets and a shuttlecock — a feathered cone that flies far and falls slow. Tap it over, and it hangs in the air like a parachute before drifting down.',
  mean:'Every ball sport asks young eyes to track something fast. Badminton does the opposite: the shuttle floats long enough for the eyes to lead, the feet to arrive and the tap to happen on purpose. It is tracking practice wearing a game.',
  know:'Your child needs three badminton ideas: the grip is a friendly handshake on the handle, not a squeeze; the tap is underarm, soft and up; and the shuttle\u2019s wobble is normal — it drifts, and drifting is its charm.',
  why:'For children who find balls too quick, badminton is the door into racket sports: the slow float buys confidence first, skill second. Many future tennis players discover their swing right here.',
  dev:'Object tracking with generous air time, underarm striking with an implement, patience between hits, and the soft-force lesson — gentler tap, prettier float.',
  vocab:'badminton, shuttlecock, tap, float, drift, net, gentle',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is badminton — the game with the feather rocket."\n2) Meet the shuttle: hold it up, drop it, watch it wobble down. "See how it floats? That is why this game is slow and lovely."\n3) Handshake grip: hold the racket like shaking hands with it — thumb wrap friendly, not knuckle-white.\n4) Tap-up drill: underarm taps, shuttle floats up, drifts down, tap again. Count the taps in a whisper — this is the quiet game.\n5) Play the drifting rally below and close: "soft tap, slow float, easy eyes."',
  say:'"Badminton\u2019s ball is a shuttlecock — a little feather rocket that flies fast then floats down slow as a leaf. We hold the racket like a handshake, tap it softly underneath, and watch it drift. The float is the game: your eyes just follow it, and tap again when it says hello."',
  notice:'On this card the word "badminton" sits big at the top with the game\u2019s scene below — rackets and the floating shuttle exactly as the slow rally looks on court.',
  ask:'"What is the ball of badminton called?" "Does the shuttle zoom or float?" "How do we hold the racket — like a hammer or a handshake?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It floats like a parachute!" "I tapped it and it drifted!" or the handshake report: "My grip is friendly, not squishy!"',
  real:'Badminton lives in gyms, gardens and family picnics — one net and two cheap rackets anywhere. Watch a rally together and point out the drift: the shuttle never falls straight, it wanders down like a leaf.',
  play:'Drift Rally: tap the shuttle over a string-net to a partner, no hurry, no score — just how long can the rally float? Add the Whisper Rule: this game is played quiet. The gentleness is the skill, and the quietness makes it land.',
  tips:'If the shuttle\u2019s wobble makes taps miss, use a sponge ball first and reintroduce the shuttle as "the fancy one." And play into open space, not over a tight net — drifting is the joy; scoring can wait a year.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we call the feather ball? Does it fall fast or float? How does the racket say hello?" Shuttlecock, float, handshake — three answers and the quiet game begins.'},
 'table-tennis':{
  title:'The Table Tennis Lesson',one:'Ping pong — the rally game shrunk to a table, where the bounces come quick and the eyes get quick too.',
  seoTitle:'Table Tennis Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Paddle Bounces and Table Rallies (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the ping-pong grip, the paddle bounce and the first table rally, with quick-bounce games for fast eyes and small spaces.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches table tennis as tennis\u2019s small, quick cousin: everything arrives sooner, everything is within one arm\u2019s reach, and the rally fits on a kitchen table.',
  what:'Table tennis (ping pong) is a net game on a table: small paddles, a light white ball that bounces once on each side, rallies that go ping-pong-ping.',
  mean:'Table tennis is the rally sport at walking speed for the body and hummingbird speed for the eyes. Because the court is one table, every miss is retrieved in one step — the game never breaks its own rhythm, and rhythm is what it teaches.',
  know:'Your child needs three table-tennis ideas: the paddle waits flat like a little wall; the ball is tapped UP first — bounce it on your own side before it crosses; and the eyes lead the hands — watch the ball, the paddle follows.',
  why:'For small hands and short arms the table is the perfect court: nothing is far, nothing is heavy, and the quick little bounces train the fastest tracking on this whole wall without a single sprint.',
  dev:'Short-range tracking, implement striking with quick timing, the bounce-tap-bounce pattern, and focused watching — eyes on the ball is a trainable muscle.',
  vocab:'table tennis, ping pong, paddle, bounce, net, rally, quick',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is table tennis — ping pong! The tiny fast game."\n2) Wall-waiting paddle: hold it flat in front, ball dropped ON it — how long can the ball rest? That flatness is the ready position.\n3) Bounce drill: tap the ball up, let it bounce on the paddle, tap again. Small taps, big counting.\n4) Table rules: your side first, then over the book-net — bounce, tap, over. Ping! Pong!\n5) Play the kitchen rally below and close: "flat paddle, quick eyes, ping pong."',
  say:'"Table tennis is ping pong! The paddle waits flat like a little wall. Tap the ball up — bounce — tap again. Then on the table: one bounce on your side, tap it over the book-net to me. Ping! Back it comes. Pong! Quick little eyes, quick little taps."',
  notice:'On this card the words "table tennis" are printed large at the top with the game\u2019s scene beneath — paddles, the small white ball and the low net exactly as the rally looks across the table.',
  ask:'"How big is the court in ping pong?" "What does the paddle do while it waits?" "What do we watch — the ball or the paddle?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The whole game is on the table!" "My paddle is a wall!" or the count: "Ping, pong, ping — I kept it going!"',
  real:'Ping pong lives in community halls, holiday parks and grandma\u2019s garage — the world\u2019s most findable sport. Watch one rally together and listen for the sound the game is named after.',
  play:'Kitchen Rally: books down the table-middle as the net, sponge ball for the first rounds. Serve by bounce-tap-over, return by watch-tap-over. Every five returns, both players shout PING PONG and wiggle. The table keeps every ball close enough to try again — rhythm first, points never.',
  tips:'A sponge or light plastic ball saves the china and the nerves for the first weeks. And let the serve bounce twice if it wants — the rules can shrink to fit the players; the rally is the prize.',
  check:'Ask: "Where is the court? What does the paddle look like while it waits? What do we watch?" The table, a flat wall, the ball — three answers and the quick game is on.'},
 baseball:{
  title:'The Baseball Lesson',one:'Hit it off the tee, run the diamond, come home — the game of crack, run and safe!',
  seoTitle:'Baseball Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Batting Swing, Throwing and Base Running (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the baseball stance, the level swing off a tee and running the bases, with cushion-diamond games for the whole family.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches baseball in its three beats — swing, run, catch — and lets the diamond grow out of cushions on the living-room floor.',
  what:'Baseball is a bat-and-glove game on a diamond: hit the ball the pitcher throws, run the four bases, and come home to score. The fielders catch it and throw it back before you get there.',
  mean:'Baseball is the turn-taking sport: one batter, everyone watching, everyone\u2019s turn comes around. For four-year-olds the diamond also draws the first map of sport — bases are places, home is where the points live.',
  know:'Your child needs three baseball ideas: the swing is LEVEL — a door swinging, not an axe falling; the batter stands side-on like a surfer watching the wave; and running the bases is a lap with stops — touch white, run to white.',
  why:'Off a tee, batting is the most satisfying strike in junior sport: the ball stands still, the swing is fully yours, and the CRACK is instant applause. Every rep rewards itself.',
  dev:'Level swinging with an implement, side-on stance, over-arm throwing to a target, and sequencing — hit, drop the bat, RUN.',
  vocab:'baseball, bat, tee, swing, base, run, home',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is baseball — the crack-and-run game."\n2) Build the stance: side-on, feet apart, bat held back over the shoulder like a door waiting to open.\n3) Swing the door: level through the ball on the tee — not down, not up, flat and whooshy. Whoosh three times with no ball.\n4) Then the tee: soft ball, real contact, CRACK — and the bat DROPS, legs go, run!\n5) Play the cushion diamond below and close: "side-on, swing level, run like wind."',
  say:'"Baseball is hit, run, safe! Stand sideways like a surfer, bat back over your shoulder — then swing the door LEVEL, crack! Drop the bat and run the bases, touch each one, all the way home. And when you play catch after — big glove, fingers up, pull the ball into the pocket."',
  notice:'On this card the word "baseball" stands at the top in big letters, the game\u2019s scene below — bat, glove and the flying ball exactly as the sport looks on the diamond.',
  ask:'"Which way does the swing go — down like an axe or around like a door?" "What do we do after the crack?" "What are the four white squares called?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Crack! I hit it!" "I ran all the bases!" or the batter\u2019s secret: "I stand like a surfer!"',
  real:'Baseball lives in parks and backyards everywhere — and its little cousin lives on every picnic blanket. Watch a few pitches together and hunt for the level swing: doors, not axes.',
  play:'Cushion Diamond: four cushions as bases around the room, a stool-tee at "home." Hit off the tee (or a gentle grown-up pitch), run touch-touch-touch, home shouts SAFE with both arms out — the fielder\u2019s favourite word. Then swap: fielder catches the next hit. Every child plays both sides, which is the whole sport.',
  tips:'Rolling a sock around the bat tip slows the swing naturally — heavy-end training, pro-style. And let the tee shrink in height over the weeks: waist, then knee — the level swing learns to find both.',
  check:'Ask: "Level swing or chopping swing? What do we drop before we run? What do we shout when we reach home?" Level, the bat, SAFE — three answers and the diamond is open.'},
 cricket:{
  title:'The Cricket Lesson',one:'The long-game — straight bat, three little stumps and an afternoon that belongs to the bowler and the batter.',
  seoTitle:'Cricket Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Straight Bat and Gentle Bowling (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the cricket grip, the straight full swing and underarm bowling, with backyard-wickets games from the nations of cricket.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches cricket around one shape: the bat swings straight through the line like a door with a hinge — and the bowling arrives slow enough to love.',
  what:'Cricket is a bat-and-ball game between two teams: the bowler bowls at three stumps, the batter defends them and hits away, then the pair run. The fielders fetch — a lot.',
  mean:'Cricket is the patient sport: turn after turn, over after over, the game breathes slowly. For a four-year-old it teaches the longest duel in sport — one bowler, one batter, and the three little sticks that matter to both.',
  know:'Your child needs three cricket ideas: the bat hangs flat like a door waiting to swing; the straight drive goes through the line of the ball; and bowling is an underarm ROLL first — big, slow and friendly.',
  why:'Cricket owns half this wall\u2019s map: India, Australia, England, New Zealand, Pakistan, the Caribbean. A four-year-old who can swing straight has joined the biggest after-school club on Earth.',
  dev:'Straight-line striking, the side-on stance with hands together, underarm rolling (the gentler sibling of throwing), and fetching — the fielding half of every bat game.',
  vocab:'cricket, bat, stumps, wickets, bowl, straight, run',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is cricket — the game with the three little sticks."\n2) Meet the stumps: three tubes standing in a row — the batter\u2019s castle. The bowler\u2019s job is to knock it; the batter\u2019s job is the wall.\n3) Grip and stance: both hands together on the flat bat, side-on, bat hanging down like a door on its hinge.\n4) The straight drive: swing the door straight through the ball\u2019s line — through the gate, not across it. Ten slow swings.\n5) Play backyard wickets below and close: "straight bat, big swing, run the runs."',
  say:'"Cricket is the three-sticks game! The stumps stand behind you like a little castle. Hold the bat flat with both hands, stand side-on, and swing STRAIGHT through the ball — whack! Then you run, and the other team fetches. And when it is your turn to bowl: big gentle underarm, right at the castle."',
  notice:'On this card the word "cricket" is printed large at the top with the game\u2019s scene underneath — the flat bat, the stumps and the red ball exactly as the game looks on the green.',
  ask:'"What stands behind the batter?" "Which way does the bat swing — across or straight through?" "How does a gentle bowler deliver the ball?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "I protected the castle!" "Whack — straight through the gate!" or the bowler\u2019s line: "Big slow underarm, watch this!"',
  real:'Cricket is everywhere from Auckland to Adelaide to Ahmedabad — the beach, the street, the park at dusk. Watch ten balls of a real match and call the shape together: straight door, straight swing.',
  play:'Backyard Wickets: three tubes as stumps, a bucket six paces away. Underarm bowl at the castle (one point), then batter swings the straight drive and the pair run one lap of the bucket (two points). Bowling earns, batting earns — the two jobs trade every over, which is cricket\u2019s whole rhythm at knee height.',
  tips:'A big soft ball and a wide bat keep early swings honest. And teach the bowler first: children who can deliver a friendly ball become the batter everyone wants — and the patience of bowling is the patience of the whole game.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we defend? Straight swing or cross swing? How does a kind bowler bowl?" The stumps, straight, underarm — three answers and the long game has begun.'},
 rugby:{
  title:'The Rugby Lesson',one:'Run forward, pass back — the wonderful backwards rule that makes running the whole game.',
  seoTitle:'Rugby Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Two-Hand Carry and the Backward Pass (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the rugby carry, the backward pass and the tag tug, with running games from the game New Zealand and Australia call their own.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches rugby around its delightful upside-down rule: the ball only travels backwards — so to score, somebody has to RUN.',
  what:'Rugby is a running game with an oval ball: carry it in both hands, run forward, and pass only backwards to teammates. Score by grounding the ball past the far line — a try!',
  mean:'Every other ball sport rewards passing forward — rugby forbids it. That one twist makes running the only way the game moves, which is why rugby players are runners first and passers second, and why the rule alone is a lesson worth an afternoon.',
  know:'Your child needs three rugby ideas: the ball is carried in TWO hands — hugged, not held; the pass goes BACKWARDS over the shoulder; and in the junior game the tackle is a tag — grab the ribbon, never the body.',
  why:'Rugby owns this wall\u2019s southern hemisphere: New Zealand\u2019s all blacks haka, Australia\u2019s league and union, South Africa\u2019s springboks. It is also the finest excuse to run Flat Out while hugging a cushion.',
  dev:'Two-hand carrying while running (a real coordination split), the around-the-corner backward pass, evasion running — and in tag versions, the honesty of the tug.',
  vocab:'rugby, try, pass, backwards, carry, tag, run',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is rugby — the hug-the-ball running game."\n2) The carry: ball (or cushion) hugged in both arms at the chest — not under one arm like a baguette. Jog a lap, hug intact.\n3) The backwards rule: demonstrate forward pass — shake the head! Then the legal one: stop, swing the ball back over the shoulder, partner walking behind catches.\n4) Join them: run forward three steps, pass back, runner receives on the move. The rhythm IS the sport.\n5) Play try-line tag below and close: "hug, run, pass it back."',
  say:'"Rugby is the backwards game! Hug the ball in both arms, run as fast as your legs go — and if you pass, it must go BACKWARDS, over your shoulder, to the friend behind you. Forward pass? No no! Run to the far wall and press the ball down — TRY!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "rugby" leads at the top in big letters with the game\u2019s scene below — the oval ball carried in both hands exactly as the run looks on the field.',
  ask:'"Can we pass the ball forward?" "How many hands carry the ball?" "What do we shout when we press it down past the line?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Backwards only — that\u2019s the rule!" "I hugged it all the way!" or the try call: "TRY! I pressed it down!"',
  real:'Rugby lives in every park in Auckland, Sydney and Cardiff — and on the screen every spring. Watch one try together and count the passes: they all travel backwards. The whole game in one rule.',
  play:'Try-Line Tag: two players with tag-ribbons tucked in the waistband, ball hugged, running the length of the room. The chaser tugs a ribbon — stop, pass BACK over the shoulder to a fresh runner, ribbons reset, go again. Cross the far line, press the ball down, TRY — and the haka dance of your family\u2019s own invention.',
  tips:'The backward pass feels wrong for about ten minutes, then it feels like the best secret in sport — let the wrongness be funny. And if a real tag belt is missing, a scarf tucked in the waistband works exactly as well.',
  check:'Ask: "Which way does a rugby pass go? How many hands on the ball? What is scoring called?" Backwards, two, a try — three answers and the running game is on.'},
 'american-football':{
  title:'The American Football Lesson',one:'The egg-shaped ball that wobbles — throw it far, hug it close, and dive for the end zone.',
  seoTitle:'American Football Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Grip, the Throw and the Touchdown (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the football grip, the over-arm throw and the touchdown dive, with end-zone games for throwing skills and big cheers.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches American football through its strangest, best toy: the pointed ball that flies funny, wobbles wonderful and demands the best over-arm throw in sport.',
  what:'American football is a running-and-throwing game on a long field: one team throws and runs the oval ball downfield, the other chases and tackles (or tags), and crossing the far line scores a touchdown.',
  mean:'The oval ball is the point of the lesson: it will not bounce straight, it will not fly straight — it demands the fingers lace the points, the arm comes over the top, and the body steps into the throw. The funniest ball teaches the finest throw.',
  know:'Your child needs three football ideas: fingers find the LACES for the grip; the throw comes over the top like a rainbow with a spin; and the end zone is the promised land — cross the line, hold the ball tight, TOUCHDOWN.',
  why:'From Super Bowl Sunday to the schoolyard at recess, this is the sport American children meet first — and its over-arm throw is the single most transferable skill on the wall: baseball throws it, tennis serves it, every dodgeball throws it.',
  dev:'Over-arm throwing at full extension, the lace grip (fine motor), running with purpose to a line, and the caught-in-the-crowd celebration — sport needs its parties.',
  vocab:'football, laces, throw, catch, end zone, touchdown, run',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is American football — the wobbly egg-ball game."\n2) Meet the ball: roll it — it curves! Bounce it — it goes sideways! The oval ball is deliciously disobedient, and that is why it needs special hands.\n3) The lace grip: fingers over the laces, point of the ball peeking past the hand. Hug-carry for runners, laces for throwers.\n4) The throw: ball to the ear, step forward, arm over the TOP — let it spin. Ten throws at a cushion target, wobbles welcome.\n5) Play the end-zone dash below and close: "hug to run, laces to throw, cross the line."',
  say:'"American football\u2019s ball is a funny egg! Put your fingers on the laces, bring it back by your ear, step and throw OVER the top — see it spin? Wobble is fine. And when you HUG it and run all the way past the far line and hold it down — that is a TOUCHDOWN, the biggest word in the whole sport!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "american football" stand large at the top with the game\u2019s scene below — the brown oval ball exactly as the sport looks on the gridiron.',
  ask:'"Why does this ball fly funny?" "Where do the fingers go?" "What do we shout when we cross the line?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It wobbles when it flies!" "My fingers found the laces!" or the big one: "TOUCHDOWN!" with both arms up.',
  real:'Football lives on every American screen from September to February — watch ONE kickoff return together and spot the three things from today: the hug-carry, the lace-throw, the line-crossing party.',
  play:'End-Zone Dash: two cushions one room apart. Quarterback throws (or hands off) to the runner, who hugs and dodges past a tagger to the far cushion — touchdown, spike the (soft) ball, dance. Swap jobs every play. The thrower throws, the runner runs, the tagger tags — three jobs, one game, everyone loud.',
  tips:'A foam football or even a sock-stuffed sock flies soft and spins true enough for the lesson. And teach the touchdown dance early — in this family, style points count.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do the fingers go for a throw? Over the top or sidearm? What is the far line called?" Laces, over the top, the end zone — three answers and the wobbly ball flies.'},
 hockey:{
  title:'The Hockey Lesson',one:'Stick low, puck sliding — the dribble-and-shoot game where the stick becomes an extra arm.',
  seoTitle:'Hockey Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Stick Handling and the Floor Dribble (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the hockey grip, the side-to-side dribble and the first shot, with floor-hockey games that need only a broom and a sponge ball.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches hockey on the floor first, where nobody slips: the stick becomes a long arm, the ball becomes a pet to herd, and the goal is always two chair-legs away.',
  what:'Hockey is a team game played with curved sticks: dribble the puck or ball side to side, pass with a push, and shoot for the goal. On ice it is the fastest team sport there is; on a living-room floor it is the same game at four-year-old speed.',
  mean:'Hockey moves the ball sideways — no other sport on this wall steers with lateral taps. That side-to-side dribble builds wrist control and the quiet discovery that a stick is a lever: push far end, ball goes near end.',
  know:'Your child needs three hockey ideas: hands split the stick — one on top, one halfway down; the dribble is little pushes SIDEWAYS, never a big whack; and the shot is one strong push through the ball toward the middle of the goal.',
  why:'Floor hockey asks for no ice, no rink and no skates — a broom and a sponge ball do it. And the dribble it teaches is the same one field hockey, ice hockey and the golf swing all borrow later.',
  dev:'Lever control (two-handed implement at split grip), lateral tapping accuracy, quick direction changes while herding, and turn-taking in goal.',
  vocab:'hockey, stick, puck, dribble, push, shoot, goal',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is hockey — the stick-and-slide game."\n2) Split the hands: one at the top of the stick, one partway down — the long arm and the steering arm.\n3) The dribble: little sideways pushes, ball stays close — a dog on a short leash. Weave between two chair-cones.\n4) The pass: a firm push with the stick\u2019s flat face to a partner\u2019s stick — hockey passes are pushes, not swings.\n5) Play chair-goal hockey below and close: "low stick, little pushes, big shot."',
  say:'"Hockey is the stick game! One hand on top, one hand down low — the stick is your extra arm. Push the ball side to side, little pushes, keep it close like a pet on a leash. Then pass it with a push, and when the goal is open — one big push, SHOOT!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "hockey" stands big at the top with the game\u2019s scene beneath — the curved sticks and the puck exactly as the game looks on the ice or the floor.',
  ask:'"Where do our two hands go on the stick?" "Big whacks or little pushes?" "What do we call the black disk the stick pushes?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The stick is my extra arm!" "I herded the ball through the cones!" or the shot call: "Push… SHOOT!"',
  real:'Hockey lives on ice rinks and in driveways — street hockey, knee hockey on carpets, field hockey on grass. Watch thirty seconds of a real game and count the sideways pushes: the dribble is the whole sport\u2019s heartbeat.',
  play:'Chair-Goal Hockey: two chair-legs as the goal, a broom or pool noodle as the stick, a sponge ball as the puck. Dribble a cone-slalom, then shoot for the chair-mouth. Swap: one shoots, one is the goalkeeper — stick flat, guard the mouth. Goals and saves both get the full roar.',
  tips:'Keep every swing below the knee — floor hockey\u2019s one rule, and it protects shins and lamps alike. A rolled-up pair of socks is the perfect first puck: it cannot roll away and cannot hurt anything.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do the hands go? Push or whack? What guards the goal?" Split grip, little pushes, the goalkeeper — three answers and the floor game is on.'},
 lacrosse:{
  title:'The Lacrosse Lesson',one:'The net-pocket game — scoop the ball up, carry it in the stick, and catch like a nest.',
  seoTitle:'Lacrosse Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Scoop, the Cradle and the Nest Catch (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the lacrosse scoop, the cradle carry and the pocket catch, with scooping games that train catching from below.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches lacrosse from the ground up — literally: the first skill is scooping the ball off the floor into a net, and for many children it is the first catch they ever make easily.',
  what:'Lacrosse is a team game with a netted pocket on the end of a stick: scoop the ball up, cradle it while you run, pass or shoot with a flick of the stick. It is the oldest sport on this wall — played first by the Haudenosaunee.',
  mean:'Every catching skill on this wall catches from the front or above. Lacrosse catches from BELOW — the ball drops into the nest. Children whose hands struggle with mid-air catches often shine the moment the stick does the catching.',
  know:'Your child needs three lacrosse ideas: the scoop is a shovel motion — stick low, through the ball, up; the cradle is a gentle rocking that keeps the ball nestling while running; and the pass is a flick, like tossing from a salad bowl.',
  why:'Lacrosse carries two gifts: a catching skill for children who need the ball to slow down, and a story worth telling — the game grew up on this continent long before any of the others arrived.',
  dev:'Scooping from the floor (ground-level tracking), carrying while moving (the cradle is balance plus focus), under-flick accuracy, and the respect-first history of the Creator\u2019s game.',
  vocab:'lacrosse, stick, net, scoop, cradle, flick, carry',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is lacrosse — the game with the net basket on a stick."\n2) The scoop: stick low like a shovel, push it through the ball along the floor, lift — the ball nests. Five scoops, celebrate each.\n3) The cradle: ball in the pocket, rock the stick gently in and out as you walk — in-out, in-out. It is a lullaby for a ball.\n4) The flick: tip the stick forward softly and toss the ball to a partner\u2019s pocket or a bucket.\n5) Play the scoop relay below and close: "scoop, cradle, flick."',
  say:'"Lacrosse sticks have a little net basket! Scoop the ball up off the floor — shloop! — nestle it in the net, and rock it gently while you walk. Cradle, cradle. Then flick it soft as tossing salad to a friend. The stick catches for you — your job is just to keep it rocking."',
  notice:'On this card the word "lacrosse" is printed large at the top with the game\u2019s scene below — the netted stick and the small ball exactly as the game looks on the field.',
  ask:'"What is in the net at the end of the stick?" "How do we pick the ball up?" "What do our arms do while we run with the ball?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The net caught it!" "I cradled it all the way across!" or the scoop cheer: "Shloop — got it!"',
  real:'Lacrosse lives in schools and clubs across Canada and the US — and its scoop lives in every kitchen: a spoon lifting cereal is a scoop, a net bag gathering toys is a cradle. The oldest sport is hiding in the chores.',
  play:'Scoop Relay: bucket at one end of the room, five scattered soft balls, stick (or large spoon) in hand. Scoop each ball, cradle it to the bucket, flick it in — five trips, timed by cheering not by clock. Then swap and race a partner gently. The floor is emptied by the nest, and every scoop is a catch.',
  tips:'If no stick exists, a milk jug cut into a scoop or a large serving spoon carries the whole lesson. And teach the cradle BEFORE the running — rocking still, then walking, then jogging: the lullaby has three speeds.',
  check:'Ask: "How do we lift the ball from the floor? What keeps it in the pocket while we run? What does the pass look like?" Scoop, the cradle, a flick — three answers and the nest is open.'},
 handball:{
  title:'The Handball Lesson',one:'Three steps, one big throw, arms up in goal — the fastest simple game on the wall.',
  seoTitle:'Handball Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Goal Throw and the Wide Keeper (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the handball run-and-throw, the one-arm goal shot and the spread-eagle save, with three-step games for strong throws.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches handball in its three-beat rhythm — run, throw, save — and turns a doorway into the most shot-at goal in the house.',
  what:'Handball is a team game where hands do everything: run with the ball (three steps at our size), bounce it if you like, then throw it into the goal. The goalkeeper stands tall with arms wide to stop it.',
  mean:'Handball\u2019s throw is the strongest one-arm throw in sport — a leap, a whip, a finish. At four-year-old scale it becomes the best throwing practice on this wall: a real target, a real keeper, and permission to throw AT something at last.',
  know:'Your child needs three handball ideas: three steps is the rule of the game — run three, throw or bounce; the goal shot is one arm, big and high, aimed at the corners; and the keeper is a starfish — feet spread, arms wide, wobble allowed.',
  why:'Every child wants to throw at a target; every parent wants the target to be soft and the rules short. Handball is that wish granted: three steps, one throw, and the world\u2019s most dramatic save pose.',
  dev:'One-arm throwing with trunk rotation (the power pattern all throws grow from), stepping to a line under excitement, and the keeper\u2019s read — watching a ball, then meeting it.',
  vocab:'handball, throw, goal, three steps, keeper, save, corner',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is handball — the run-three-steps-and-throw game."\n2) The three-step rule: walk it out loud — step, step, step… THROW or BOUNCE. The rule is the game\u2019s heartbeat; practise it dry first.\n3) The goal shot: one arm, big circle back, step into it, finish pointing at the corner you chose. Corners beat middles — that is the aiming lesson.\n4) The starfish save: feet wide, arms up like a big X, body behind the ball. Practise three saves from gentle throws.\n5) Play doorway handball below and close: "three steps, one throw, starfish saves."',
  say:'"Handball is the three-step game! Run step-step-step — no more! — then one BIG throw with one arm at the goal. Pick your corner, not the middle. And when you are the keeper: feet wide, arms up, be a starfish! Every save is worth a goal, and every goal is worth a starfish."',
  notice:'On this card the word "handball" leads at the top in big letters with the game\u2019s scene underneath — the flying throw at the goal exactly as the sport looks on the court.',
  ask:'"How many steps can we run?" "One arm or two for the goal shot?" "What shape is the goalkeeper?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Three steps, then I throw!" "I hit the top corner!" or the keeper\u2019s cry: "STARFISH — saved it!"',
  real:'Handball owns playgrounds across Europe — and its three-step spirit lives in every hallway game of throw-the-sock-into-the-hamper. Watch one fast-break goal together and count the steps: step, step, step, throw.',
  play:'Doorway Handball: the doorway (or two cushion towers) is the goal, a soft ball, keeper in starfish. Attacker runs three steps from the corridor and throws for a corner; keeper saves or concedes with full drama. Trade places every throw. Three rules total: three steps, pads-soft ball, and the keeper may wobble.',
  tips:'Corners are hard to hit and easy to love — praise the AIMED miss more than the lucky middle. And let the keeper lie down dramatically after each goal; goalkeeping is theatre, and four-year-olds know it.',
  check:'Ask: "How many steps before we throw? Which arm throws at goal? What is the keeper\u2019s shape?" Three, one arm, a starfish — three answers and the doorway is a stadium.'},
 golf:{
  title:'The Golf Lesson',one:'The small ball, the slow swing, the counting game — aiming made gentle.',
  seoTitle:'Golf Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Slow Swing and the Counting Game (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the golf grip, the smooth pendulum swing and stroke counting, with carpet-golf games that make aiming quiet and fun.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches golf as the wall\u2019s whisper: where hockey pushes and baseball whips, golf swings SLOW — and counts every tap on the way to the hole.',
  what:'Golf is a target game on a very big course: swing the club, send the small ball toward the hole, and count how many strokes it takes. Fewest taps wins — and slow swings beat strong ones every time.',
  mean:'Golf is the control sport. Every other card rewards force; golf rewards restraint — the smoother the swing, the straighter the ball. For a four-year-old that is a brand-new idea: sometimes the body wins by slowing down.',
  know:'Your child needs three golf ideas: hands hold the club like two friends sharing one handle; the swing is a pendulum — back slow, through slower; and every tap is a COUNT — the counting is not extra, it is the game.',
  why:'Aiming small and swinging slow grows the precision end of fundamental movement skills — the twin of the big-power skills every other ball game drills. Golf is where the body learns whisper control.',
  dev:'Pendulum swinging (pendulum rhythm is a timing skill all its own), small-target aiming, stroke counting — early maths on the fairway — and the patience of trying again calmly.',
  vocab:'golf, club, swing, putt, hole, stroke, count',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is golf — the slow-swing counting game."\n2) The grip: both hands together on the handle, thumbs down the middle — two friends sharing an umbrella.\n3) The pendulum: swing back like a clock hand… and through like a clock hand — smooth, no chopping. Whoooosh, not WHACK.\n4) Putt first: the tiny swing on the carpet, ball rolling to a cup. Count every tap out loud: one… two… three — in the cup!\n5) Play carpet golf below and close: "slow swing, small ball, keep counting."',
  say:'"Golf is the whisper game! Hold the club like two friends sharing one umbrella. Swing slow — back like a clock, through like a clock, whoooosh. The tiny ball rolls and rolls… and we count every tap: one, two, THREE — in the hole! Fewest taps wins, and slow is the secret."',
  notice:'On this card the word "golf" stands at the top in big letters with the game\u2019s scene below — the club, the small white ball and the green exactly as the game looks on the course.',
  ask:'"Fast swing or slow swing?" "What do we count?" "How do our hands hold the club?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Whoosh — slow like a clock!" "Three taps and I sank it!" or the golfer\u2019s whisper: "Gentle… gentle… in!"',
  real:'Golf lives at courses, driving ranges and mini-golf parks — the mini-putt course is a child\u2019s paradise built exactly on today\u2019s skill. Walk one together and count strokes per hole like the pros.',
  play:'Carpet Golf: a bucket or cup at one end of the carpet, a spoon-club, a pom-pom ball. Stroke one: tap. Stroke two: tap. Sink it in three — par for the living room! Lay a "course" of three cups with sofa-cushion hazards between. The scorecard can be drawn; the counting must be loud.',
  tips:'Make the child the scoreboard — counting strokes is maths practice smuggled into sport. And if the swing keeps chopping, hold a balloon above the clubhead: a swing that pops it is chopping; a swing that never touches is a true pendulum.',
  check:'Ask: "Whack or whoosh? What do we count after every tap? What wins the game?" Whoosh, the strokes, fewest taps — three answers and the whisper game is on.'},
 athletics:{
  title:'The Athletics Lesson',one:'Run fast, jump far, throw strong — the three first skills of every body, done as sport.',
  seoTitle:'Athletics Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Run, Jump and Throw (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the athletic sprint start, the two-foot long jump and the bean-bag throw — the run-jump-throw heart of fundamental movement skills.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches athletics in its three honest words — run, jump, throw — the exact three New Zealand and Australian schools call the heart of movement, and the three every four-year-old is already in love with.',
  what:'Athletics is track and field: sprints and distance running on the track, long jumps and high jumps in the field, throws of every weight — all of it measured, all of it cheered, all of it built from skills children meet in the backyard first.',
  mean:'Athletics is the mother of sports: nearly every ball game on this wall borrows its run, its jump or its throw. Done on its own, it needs no team, no ball and no rules longer than a breath — the purest gross motor sport there is.',
  know:'Your child needs three athletics ideas: the sprint begins with a crouch and a WAIT for the word go; the long jump is knees bent, arms swing, TWO feet off together; and the throw turns the whole body side-on and lets the arm arrive last.',
  why:'Run, jump, throw is the exact phrase NZ\u2019s and Australia\u2019s school curriculums use for fundamental movement skills — teaching the three words explicitly gives a child the keys to every sport that follows.',
  dev:'Sprint acceleration, two-foot take-off with arm swing, side-on throwing with trunk rotation — the three pillars — plus measuring: how far is a really good day?',
  vocab:'athletics, sprint, jump, throw, track, field, measure',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is athletics — running, jumping and throwing, the Olympic games."\n2) The sprint: crouch low, fingers on the line, freeze… then GO on the word. Practise the waiting — the crouch is half the race.\n3) The long jump: bend the knees, swing the arms BACK then UP, two feet leave together, land like a frog. Measure each jump with a shoe.\n4) The throw: stand side-on, weight back, big arm circle, release pointing at the sky\u2019s window — measure with footsteps.\n5) Run the three-station mini-meet below and close: "crouch, bend, turn — go!"',
  say:'"Athletics is three games in one! Crouch down for the sprint — wait for GO, then fly. Bend your knees for the long jump — swing the arms, two feet, FROG! Then turn sideways for the throw — big circle, let it fly, how many footsteps did it go? Run, jump, throw: that is the Olympics of the backyard."',
  notice:'On this card the word "athletics" is printed big at the top with the game\u2019s scene beneath — the track, the lane lines and the runner exactly as a race day looks.',
  ask:'"What do we do while we wait at the sprint line?" "Do we jump off one foot or two?" "Which way does our body face for a big throw?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "I waited… then ZOOM!" "My frog jump went two shoes!" or the thrower\u2019s report: "It flew six footsteps!"',
  real:'Athletics owns every school sports day in the US, Canada, Australia and NZ — and the Olympics bring the whole wall to life every four years. Watch one long jump together and find the three beats: bend, swing, frog.',
  play:'Three-Station Meet: chalk a sprint lane, a jump line and a throw zone. One minute at each — sprint out and back, three frog jumps measured in shoes, one mighty bean-bag throw measured in footsteps. Write the "records" on paper, cheer absurdly, and hand out a drawn medal. That is a real athletics meet, scaled for four legs.',
  tips:'Measure everything — shoes, footsteps, books. The measuring is what turns running around into ATHLETICS, and comparing your own record to yesterday\u2019s is the healthiest competition there is.',
  check:'Ask: "What are the three athletics words? What waits at the sprint line? How do we land the long jump?" Run-jump-throw, a crouch, like a frog — three answers and the meet is open.'},
 gymnastics:{
  title:'The Gymnastics Lesson',one:'Shapes, balances and rolls — the sport of telling your body exactly what to do.',
  seoTitle:'Gymnastics Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Balance, Shapes and Safe Rolls (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the gymnastics balance, the star and pencil shapes and the log roll, with living-room mat games for body control.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches gymnastics as the wall\u2019s quiet king: no ball, no net, no goal — just a body learning to make shapes and hold them still.',
  what:'Gymnastics is the sport of body control: balances on feet and hands, shapes made with straight arms and pointed toes, rolls across the mats, and swings on bars — every move practised slowly until it looks easy.',
  mean:'Balance is the skill every other sport silently borrows — the skater, the batter, the karate kid all stand on one leg in the end. Gymnastics trains it on purpose, slowly, which is why gymnasts pick up every other game faster.',
  know:'Your child needs three gymnastics ideas: the star shape is arms AND legs wide, held like a superhero landing; the log roll keeps the body STRAIGHT while it turns; and the balance asks for stillness — wobble is part of the work, not the failure.',
  why:'Of everything on this wall, gymnastics is the most transferable hour a four-year-old can spend: grip, balance, falling safely and the bravery of trying a new shape all live here before anywhere else.',
  dev:'Static balance, body-shape awareness (straight, tucked, wide), rolling — the first safe-fall skill — and the focus of holding still while everything wants to wiggle.',
  vocab:'gymnastics, balance, shape, roll, still, hold, mat',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is gymnastics — the game of making shapes with your body."\n2) The star: arms up wide, legs apart, hold for three slow counts — superhero landing, eyes forward. Wobbling counts as trying.\n3) The pencil: body STRAIGHT, arms squeezed to the ears, stand on tiptoe — the tallest shape there is.\n4) The log roll: lie straight as a pencil on the bed or mat, arms up, and roll across — the body never bends. Three rolls, giggles mandatory.\n5) Play shape-freeze below and close: "make the shape, hold it still, ta-da."',
  say:'"Gymnastics is making your body into shapes! Star shape — arms wide, legs wide, hold it like a superhero. Pencil shape — body straight, arms squeezed past your ears, tippy-toes. And the log roll — straight as a pencil, roll across the mat like a falling tree. Wobbles are allowed; stillness is the treasure."',
  notice:'On this card the word "gymnastics" leads at the top in big letters with the game\u2019s scene underneath — the balancing child with arms out exactly as a class on the mats looks.',
  ask:'"What shapes can your body make?" "What does the log roll keep straight?" "Is wobbling okay?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "I held the star for five!" "I rolled like a pencil!" or the gymnast\u2019s bow: "Ta-da!"',
  real:'Gymnastics lives in every soft-play centre and school hall — and its shapes live in every living room: the superhero landing, the tip-toe reach for the biscuit tin, the log roll across the big bed. Shapes everywhere.',
  play:'Shape-Freeze: call a shape — STAR! PENCIL! TUCK (knees hugged)! — and freeze in it. Three counts of stillness earns a point; a giggle-collapse earns a retry. Then the balance beam: a line of tape on the floor, heel-to-toe, arms out, and a proper gymnast finish — arms up, one knee, ta-da. The living room becomes the podium.',
  tips:'Practise on something soft and celebrate the wobble — stillness grows out of wobbles, never out of scolding. And teach the safety roll (chin tucked, roll on shoulders) as THE way to fall; it is judo\u2019s gift hiding inside gymnastics.',
  check:'Ask: "Name a gymnastics shape. What stays straight in a log roll? Is it better to hold still or wiggle?" Star (or pencil), the body, hold still — three answers and the mats are open.'},
 marathon:{
  title:'The Marathon Lesson',one:'The longest race of all — where the win is one more step, and one more, and one more.',
  seoTitle:'Marathon Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Pacing, Stamina and Keep-Going Power (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach pacing, steady steps and the joy of finishing, with lap-counting marathon games that build stamina gently.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the marathon\u2019s one big secret: the race is not won by the fastest legs but by the legs that never stop — pacing is a skill, and four is a fine age to start it.',
  what:'A marathon is a running race of 42.195 kilometres — a whole day on your feet. Nobody sprints it: marathoners jog at one steady speed for hours, drinking water at little tables, cheered by whole cities, and finishing is the victory.',
  mean:'Four-year-olds are sprinters by nature: all burst, no battery. The marathon card teaches the opposite muscle — the keep-going muscle — and it does it in the friendliest unit there is: one lap, then one more.',
  know:'Your child needs three marathon ideas: marathoners go SLOW on purpose — steady beats speedy when the road is long; water breaks are part of the race, not a timeout from it; and the finish line is for EVERYONE who keeps stepping.',
  why:'Stamina is the quietest fundamental movement skill — hidden inside every long playground morning. Naming it, counting laps and celebrating the finish turns I\u2019m tired into one more step, which is a life skill wearing a race bib.',
  dev:'Aerobic stamina in tiny doses, pace self-regulation (the hardest skill in the lesson), lap counting, and the finish-line feeling — effort followed by enormous pride.',
  vocab:'marathon, race, pace, steady, lap, finish line, medal',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is the marathon — the longest race in the world."\n2) The distance: 42 kilometres — walk it out: "that is walking ALL DAY." Ask what you would need to bring. (Water! Shoes! Patience!)\n3) The pace secret: sprint across the room — done in five seconds, breath gone. Then jog-slow the same room — still going. THAT is marathon running: slow enough to keep going.\n4) The water table: marathoners drink WHILE racing — practise the funny walk-and-sip.\n5) Play the living-room marathon below and close: "steady steps, one more, finish line."',
  say:'"The marathon is the longest race there is — 42 kilometres, a whole day of running! Nobody rushes it. Steady steps, one foot, other foot, sip of water, keep going. And when you finally cross the finish line — even in last place — the whole city cheers, because you KEPT GOING. That is the marathon\u2019s secret: finishing is winning."',
  notice:'On this card the word "marathon" stands big at the top with the race\u2019s scene below — the runner, the road and the finish exactly as the long race looks on the day.',
  ask:'"Is a marathon short or long?" "Do marathoners run fast the whole way?" "What do they drink on the way?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Slow and steady keeps going!" "I did twelve laps!" or the finish call: "I made it — where is my medal?"',
  real:'Marathons close real streets in real cities — New York, Boston, Auckland, Sydney — and the crowds cheer every single finisher. Watch the last runner cross a real finish line together: the loudest cheer of the day belongs to the last pair of legs.',
  play:'Living-Room Marathon: pick a lap (hallway loop or garden circuit), agree a steady pace — no sprinting, that is the one rule — and count laps out loud. Five laps: water break at the "table." Ten laps: finish line of two chairs, tape across, break through it, medal drawn on the spot. The stamina is small, the ceremony is huge.',
  tips:'Keep laps tiny and ceremony enormous — stamina grows on reps of pride, not distance. And if the child suddenly sprints and bonks, that IS the lesson: ask how their legs feel now versus the steady laps. The body explains pacing better than any lecture.',
  check:'Ask: "How long is a marathon? What is the secret speed? What happens when you finish the race?" Very long, slow and steady, a big cheer and a medal — three answers and the keep-going muscle grows.'},
 triathlon:{
  title:'The Triathlon Lesson',one:'Swim, bike, run — three sports, one race, no stopping in between.',
  seoTitle:'Triathlon Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Swim-Bike-Run and Skill Switching (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the triathlon\u2019s three legs and the transitions between them, with a living-room race that mixes gross motor skills.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the triathlon\u2019s wonderful idea — three sports stacked into one race — and practises the strangest skill in it: changing from one kind of moving to another without stopping.',
  what:'A triathlon is one race with three legs: swim first, then cycle, then run to the finish. In between are the transitions — where athletes swap goggles for helmets for shoes as fast as they can.',
  mean:'The triathlon\u2019s hidden skill is the switch: arms doing crawl one minute, then steering handlebars, then pumping as legs. Switching movement styles mid-game is real coordination training — the brain rebuilds its plan at speed.',
  know:'Your child needs three triathlon ideas: the order never changes — swim, bike, run; the transition is part of the race (no sitting around in the middle!); and tri means three — the word itself is a lesson.',
  why:'Of all the cards on the wall, this one mixes the most skills into one game — crawl arms, pedalling legs, sprinting feet — which makes it the perfect grand finale for a morning of movement play.',
  dev:'Skill-switching (the real star), sequencing a multi-part plan, transition speed, and the endurance of doing three things before asking for a snack.',
  vocab:'triathlon, three, swim, bike, run, transition, finish',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is the triathlon — three races in one."\n2) Decode the word: TRI means three — triangle, tricycle, triathlon. What comes in threes?\n3) The three legs: swim arms (big windmills), bike legs (round and round), run feet (fast and light). Practise each for ten seconds.\n4) The transition: the magic moment — arms stop swimming, hands grab handlebars. Announce it like a race commentator: "AND SHE\u2019S ON THE BIKE!"\n5) Play the living-room triathlon below and close: "swim, bike, run — nothing stops in between."',
  say:'"The triathlon is three races glued together! First SWIM — windmill arms through the sofa-sea. Then BIKE — pedalling legs round and round. Then RUN — light feet to the finish! And the funniest part is the swap in the middle, the transition: goggles off, helmet on, GO GO GO!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "triathlon" is printed large at the top with the race\u2019s scene below — the swim, the bike and the run exactly as the three-leg race looks.',
  ask:'"What does TRI mean?" "What are the three legs, in order?" "What is the swap in the middle called?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Swim, then bike, then run!" "I did all three!" or the commentator\u2019s cry: "AND SHE\u2019S ON THE BIKE!"',
  real:'Triathlons happen in every city with a lake and a road — and kids\u2019 versions (splash, pedal, dash) exist in most towns each summer. Watch one transition zone together: shoes, helmets, chaos — the funniest minute in sport.',
  play:'Living-Room Triathlon: sofa cushions are the sea (crawl on tummies), a hobby-horse or trike loop is the bike leg, and the corridor is the run to the finish tape. Commentate every leg, time the transitions, medal the finisher. Add a second competitor for a real race — the toys may enter.',
  tips:'Keep each leg SHORT — the joy is in the switching, not the distance. And let the child be the commentator once: shouting "AND SHE\u2019S ON THE BIKE" teaches the sequence better than any diagram.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the three legs in order. What do we call the swap? What does tri mean?" Swim-bike-run, the transition, three — three answers and the race is on.'},
 cycling:{
  title:'The Cycling Lesson',one:'Helmet on, pedals round — the first machine a small person gets to drive.',
  seoTitle:'Cycling Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Pedalling, Balance and Helmet Safety (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the pedalling motion, the balance-bike glide and the helmet habit, with air-cycling games that make real pedals feel known.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches cycling from the pedal up: the round-and-round motion the legs must learn, the glide the balance bike gives for free, and the helmet rule that starts with the very first ride.',
  what:'Cycling is riding a two-wheeled machine: push the pedals round, steer with the handlebars, and balance on two wheels while the world rolls past. It is also every child\u2019s first taste of real speed under their own power.',
  mean:'The pedal stroke is a genuine coordination puzzle: one leg pushes down while the other lifts up, both going in CIRCLES — not the up-down stomping legs want to do. Training the circle first is the whole trick.',
  know:'Your child needs three cycling ideas: pedals go in CIRCLES — round and round, never stampy-stampy; the glide is the secret inside every bike — scoot, lift the feet, WHOOSH; and the helmet goes on before the wheels do, every single time.',
  why:'Cycling is the first freedom machine: the day the child outruns the grown-up is a family memory. And the safety habit worn at four — helmet, every ride — is the same habit that keeps riders safe at fourteen.',
  dev:'Circular leg coordination (a distinct skill from walking), dynamic balance via gliding, steering-hands and looking-eyes working together, and the non-negotiable safety routine.',
  vocab:'cycling, bike, pedal, helmet, balance, glide, steer',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is cycling — the machine YOU drive."\n2) Air-cycling: lie on the back, legs in the air, pedal round and round like riding an invisible bike — circles, not stamps. Sing a pedalling song if one appears.\n3) The glide: on a balance bike (or any scoot), push with the feet, then LIFT them — the bike glides on its own. Glide is balance\u2019s teacher.\n4) The helmet ritual: helmet ON before wheels touch ground, buckle clicked, two-finger check under the chin. Make it the same ritual every time — a ceremony, not a nag.\n5) Play the pedal-circle game below and close: "circles, glide, helmet on."',
  say:'"Cycling is driving your very own machine! First the helmet — click, that is the rule, every ride. Then the pedals: round and ROUND in circles, like stirring a giant pot with your feet. And when the bike starts to glide — scoot, feet up, WHOOSH — that is you, balancing all by yourself, going faster than your grown-up!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "cycling" leads at the top in big letters with the ride\u2019s scene below — the child, the bike and the helmet exactly as the first ride looks.',
  ask:'"Which way do pedals go — circles or stamps?" "What goes on your head before every ride?" "What happens when you lift your feet and let the bike roll?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Round and round, not stampy!" "Helmet clicked!" or the glide report: "I glided ALL the way to the gate!"',
  real:'Bikes live in every garage, driveway and park path in the four countries this class calls home — and the Tour de France brings the big version to the screen each July. Watch thirty seconds together and find the circles: round and round, even uphill.',
  play:'Pedal-Circle Race: two racers lie back-to-back, legs in the air, and air-cycle while a song plays — the pedal that keeps CIRCLES the longest wins (stamping is out!). Then outside or down the hall: balance-bike glide course — scoot, lift, glide between two cones, longest glide is champion. Ceremony for the champion: helmet worn, arms raised, bell rung.',
  tips:'If pedals frustrate, park them: balance-bike gliding teaches ALL the balance and none of the stress — many children pedal within a week of mastering the glide. And the helmet ritual works best when the grown-up\u2019s helmet clicks too, every time.',
  check:'Ask: "What shape do our legs make on the pedals? What do we wear every ride? What is the magic move on a balance bike?" Circles, a helmet, the glide — three answers and the wheels are ready.'},
 swimming:{
  title:'The Swimming Lesson',one:'Kick, reach, bubble — the sport that is also a life skill, learned one splash at a time.',
  seoTitle:'Swimming Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Kicks, Arm Circles and Water Safety (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the flutter kick, the windmill arms and the bubble blow, with dry-land and bath games plus the water-safety rule every family repeats.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches swimming in three dry-land pieces — kick, reach, bubble — so that the pool, when it comes, is a reunion instead of a surprise.',
  what:'Swimming is moving through water: legs flutter-kick, arms windmill-reach, and the face learns to blow bubbles instead of breathing in. It is a sport, a life skill, and the number one safety skill a small person can own.',
  mean:'Water is a whole new world for a body: it holds you up, slows you down and gets up your nose. Breaking swimming into three dry-land pieces lets the body learn each one where it is calm — the water then just joins the pieces together.',
  know:'Your child needs three swimming ideas: the kick comes from the HIP, small and fast — splashy little flutters, not big slow stomps; the arms are windmills — big slow circles that reach far; and the mouth blows bubbles — out into the water, never in.',
  why:'Swim schools across the US, Canada, Australia and NZ all start the same way — kick, reach, bubble — because those three pieces ARE swimming. And the safety rule sits underneath every lesson: water is fun WITH a grown-up, never alone.',
  dev:'Hip-driven kicking, cross-pattern arm circles (the crawl pattern), breath control (blowing out is a real self-regulation skill), and the safety habit of checking for the grown-up before every splash.',
  vocab:'swimming, kick, reach, bubble, goggles, float, water safety',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is swimming — moving through the water."\n2) The flutter kick: lie on the tummy on a bed or mat, legs out — little fast kicks from the HIP, splashy as a dolphin tail. Ten seconds of kicking to a song.\n3) The windmill arms: standing, big slow arm circles that REACH as far as they can — reach, pull, reach, pull. Then both arms take turns like a wheel.\n4) The bubble: practise on dry land — big breath in, hum and blow OUT slowly. In the bath: chin in, blow bubbles. Mouth out-in, never in-in.\n5) Say the safety rule together, then play the swim-school game below: "never swim without a grown-up."',
  say:'"Swimming is how we move through water! Little fast kicks from your hips — splish splash splish. Big windmill arms that reach far far forward. And when your face goes in — blow bubbles OUT, big breath first. And the most important rule in the whole world of water: we NEVER swim without a grown-up. Never never. Say it with me."',
  notice:'On this card the word "swimming" stands at the top in big letters with the water\u2019s scene below — the swimmer, the goggles and the blue exactly as a lesson day at the pool looks.',
  ask:'"Where does the kick start — knees or hips?" "What shape do the arms make?" "What do we blow in the water?" "And who do we NEVER swim without?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Kick kick kick — splashy!" "I blowed bubbles!" or the safety vow: "Never without a grown-up!"',
  real:'Swimming lives at pools, beaches, lakes and the bathtub. Visit the pool and hunt for the three pieces in real swimmers: flutter kicks, windmill arms, bubble streams. The bathtub is the nightly training ground — bubbles on command.',
  play:'Swim School (dry land): towel "towelie" on the head, then the three stations — flutter kick lying down, windmill arms standing, bubble-breath humming. Then the bath or shallow pool: kick the water wild, blow three bubbles, float like a starfish with grown-up hands under the back. Every session ends with the rule, whispered like a spell: never swim without a grown-up.',
  tips:'Blowing OUT is the skill inside the bubble — children who can hum-and-blow on land transfer it to water within a visit. And let the goggles do their magic: a child who can see underwater stays twice as long, learns twice as fast.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the flutter kick come from? What do the arms draw? What do we blow? Who do we never swim without?" Hips, windmills, bubbles, a grown-up — four answers and the water is a friend.'},
 diving:{
  title:'The Diving Lesson',one:'Straight as a pencil, hands first, splash small — the bravest entry in sport.',
  seoTitle:'Diving Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Pencil Shape and the Splash-Free Entry (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the diver\u2019s pencil shape, the hands-first entry and the safe pool rule, with dry-land diving games on cushions.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches diving where every real diving lesson starts: on dry land, making the shape — arms squeezing ears, body straight as a pencil — long before any water is near.',
  what:'Diving is entering the water hands first, body straight and tight, so the splash is tiny and the entry is clean. From the poolside hop to the ten-metre tower, every dive is the same beautiful shape in the air.',
  mean:'The pencil shape is a body-control marvel: straight arms squeezed to ears, tight tummy, pointed toes, held while the whole body moves. It is gymnastics\u2019 shapes with courage added — and it is 100% teachable on the carpet.',
  know:'Your child needs three diving ideas: the shape is a pencil — arms locked past the ears, body long, toes pointed; hands enter FIRST and the rest of the body follows through the same hole; and diving only ever happens in DEEP water, where a grown-up says it is allowed.',
  why:'The pencil shape doubles as the safest fall in sport and the proudest pose on the poolside — and building it on cushions makes the courage grow at home, at four, with zero water risk.',
  dev:'Body alignment under movement (the straightest line the body ever makes), courage sequencing — shape, hold, commit — plus the deep-water-only rule that keeps diving safe.',
  vocab:'diving, pencil, straight, entry, splash, deep water, brave',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is diving — going into the water hands first, straight as a pencil."\n2) Build the pencil: arms up, hands together, squeeze them past the ears, body tall, tummy tight, tippy-toes. Hold three counts. That shape IS the dive.\n3) The entry order: hands first — the pencil goes in the water like a dart, not like a starfish flop.\n4) Dry-land dive: stand on a cushion "platform," make the pencil, lean slowly forward, and fall straight as a dart onto the sofa. Shape held all the way down, every time.\n5) Say the deep-water rule, then play the dive-school game below: "diving is for deep water, with a grown-up."',
  say:'"Diving is entering the water like a dart! Arms squeeze your ears, body straight as a pencil, tippy-toes — hands go in FIRST and the whole body follows through the same little hole. Splash? What splash! And the diving rule: only in DEEP water, only when a grown-up says — shallow water is where heads bump. Say the rule with me."',
  notice:'On this card the word "diving" stands big at the top with the pool\u2019s scene below — the diver straight as an arrow above the water exactly as the entry looks from the poolside.',
  ask:'"What shape does a diver make?" "What goes into the water first?" "Where is diving allowed — shallow or deep water?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Pencil shape — arms past my ears!" "Hands first, then whoosh!" or the safety vow: "Only deep water, only with a grown-up!"',
  real:'Diving lives at every pool with a deep end — and on the Olympics\u2019 most beautiful screens. Watch one slow-motion dive together and find the pencil: arms past ears, toes pointed, one straight line from fingers to splash.',
  play:'Dive School (dry land): cushion platform, sofa "water." Make the pencil, hold it, lean, dive straight — held the whole way. Score the shape, not the landing: straight arms past ears? Toes pointed? Then the pool, someday soon, returns a dart-clean entry the shape already knows.',
  tips:'Never rush the water part — the cushion dive holds the whole lesson, and children who own the shape on land find it again in the pool the first time a teacher says pencil. The deep-water rule is non-negotiable and belongs in every repetition, out loud.',
  check:'Ask: "What shape is the dive? What enters first? Where is diving allowed?" The pencil, the hands, deep water with a grown-up — three answers and the shape is ready.'},
 'water-polo':{
  title:'The Water Polo Lesson',one:'A team floating in the deep end, throwing for the goal — legs kicking underneath, hands passing above.',
  seoTitle:'Water Polo Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Treading Water and Team Throws (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the egg-beater leg whir, the sculling hands and the one-arm pool throw, with bath-and-shallow games for future polo players.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches water polo\u2019s magic trick: hands busy passing while the LEGS do all the floating — the egg-beater whir that keeps a whole team upright in deep water.',
  what:'Water polo is a team ball game played floating: tread water, pass with one hand, shoot at the goal, sprint back. The legs whirl like an egg-beater under the surface while the arms do the busy work.',
  mean:'Water polo splits the body\u2019s jobs in half — legs float, arms play — which is exactly the split swimmers need to grow. Treading water with hands out of the water is one of the deepest water-confidence skills there is.',
  know:'Your child needs three water-polo ideas: the egg-beater is legs whirling in little circles under you, like stirring two pots; the scull is hands sweeping out-and-in at the sides, pressing the water down; and the pass is ONE hand — a throw, not a push.',
  why:'Treading water is the hidden life skill inside the sport — the skill that lets a swimmer stop, look around and rest in deep water. Polo teaches it while playing catch, which is the gentlest way a scary skill was ever learned.',
  dev:'Egg-beater leg coordination (a new pattern distinct from the flutter kick), sculling hand pressure, one-arm throwing from floating, and deep-water confidence in games-sized doses.',
  vocab:'water polo, tread, egg-beater, scull, pass, goal, deep end',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is water polo — catch and throw while you FLOAT."\n2) The egg-beater: sitting up in the bath or shallow pool, legs whirl little circles beneath — stir, stir — while the chest stays proud. Practise dry first: sit on the bed edge, legs whirling.\n3) The scull: arms out at the sides, sweep the water out and in, press DOWN to lift the chest. It is how the hands help the legs float.\n4) The one-hand pass: throw (not push) a soft ball to a partner\u2019s chest — one arm, the other one sculling.\n5) Say the deep-water rule, then play the floating pass game below: "legs whirl, hands play."',
  say:'"Water polo is catch-and-throw while you float! Your legs whirl underneath like an egg-beater — stir stir stir — and your hands sweep the water down, down, to hold you up. Then one hand grabs the ball and THROWS it to your teammate. Legs do the floating, hands do the playing — that is the whole secret. And we learn it in shallow water first, always with a grown-up."',
  notice:'On this card the words "water polo" stand large at the top with the pool\u2019s scene below — the floating players and the flying ball exactly as the game looks in the deep end.',
  ask:'"What are the legs doing while the hands play?" "Which hand throws the pass?" "Where does the body stay while all this happens?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "My legs are an egg-beater!" "I threw with ONE hand!" or the proud float: "My hands played while my legs floated!"',
  real:'Water polo lives in every city pool with a deep end — and its egg-beater lives at every swim school as the deep-water safety skill. In the bath tonight: legs whirling, chest proud, hands sweeping — the whole sport, knee-deep.',
  play:'Floating Pass: in shallow water (or the bath), sit tall with legs whirling and hands sculling; a partner tosses soft balls one at a time — catch, one-hand pass back. Five catches each, then swap thrower and floater. Dry-land version: bed-edge egg-beater legs, cushion passes. The legs learn to hold, the hands learn to play — the split that makes deep water friendly.',
  tips:'Practise the egg-beater dry, again and again — the pattern is odd enough that land reps pay off fast in water. And keep the deep-water rule attached to every mention of the deep end: shallow first, grown-up always, deep someday with the whir already humming.',
  check:'Ask: "What are the legs doing under the water? What do the hands do at the sides? How many hands pass the ball? Who do we learn in the deep end with?" Egg-beater, sculling, one, a grown-up — four answers and the deep end gets closer.'},
 surfing:{
  title:'The Surfing Lesson',one:'Paddle out, pop up, ride it in — standing tall on moving water.',
  seoTitle:'Surfing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Pop-Up and the Wobbly Balance (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the surfer\u2019s pop-up, the low knee-bent stance and the board safety rule, with carpet pop-up and cushion-wave games.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches surfing where every surf school starts: on the floor, doing the pop-up — the one jump that turns a paddler into a rider — long before the ocean gets a vote.',
  what:'Surfing is riding a board on a moving wave: paddle out past the break, turn, paddle hard as the wave lifts you, then POP UP to standing and ride it to the sand.',
  mean:'The pop-up is a push-up that ends in a crouch — one explosive move from lying to standing. Practised on the carpet it builds the exact arm push, foot swap and low stance that real waves demand, with zero salt water required.',
  know:'Your child needs three surfing ideas: the pop-up is hands-down, feet-in, one hop — like a frog with a plan; the stance is sideways, knees soft, arms out like a tightrope walker; and the leash goes on the ankle first — the board is your buddy, never let it fly free.',
  why:'Surfing is the balance card with adventure attached: the floor is never still, and the child who can pop up and hold a low stance on land owns the sport\u2019s whole first lesson before the first wave.',
  dev:'Explosive push-to-stand (the pop-up is a genuine power move), low dynamic balance, side-on stance awareness, and the ocean respect that every beach family teaches: waves are strong, grown-ups watch, never turn your back.',
  vocab:'surfing, wave, paddle, pop up, stance, balance, leash',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is surfing — standing on a board on a moving wave."\n2) Paddle first: lie on the tummy, one arm at a time reaching far forward — paddle, paddle, paddle. This is how surfers travel.\n3) The pop-up: hands flat under the shoulders, push UP, jump the feet in and land LOW — knees bent, sideways stance, arms out. A frog with a plan. Ten pop-ups, slow ones fine.\n4) Hold the ride: once up, FREEZE in the low stance for three counts — that freeze is the ride.\n5) Say the beach rules, then play the wave game below: "pop up, ride low, leash on."',
  say:'"Surfing is standing on the water! Paddle out with big arm reaches — paddle, paddle. When the wave lifts the board, POP UP: hands down, feet in, jump low! Knees soft, arms out, ride the wave all the way in. And the beach rules: the leash goes on your ankle so the board never flies away, and we always surf where the grown-ups watch."',
  notice:'On this card the word "surfing" is printed large at the top with the wave\u2019s scene underneath — the rider on the board and the blue wave exactly as the ride looks from the sand.',
  ask:'"What do we do before the pop-up?" "Are the knees stiff or soft?" "What goes on the ankle, and why?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Paddle paddle — POP UP!" "I held my ride for three!" or the beach rule: "Leash on — the board is my buddy!"',
  real:'Surfing lives at every beach break in Australia, NZ and California — and kids\u2019 board lessons start with exactly this carpet pop-up. Watch one surfer catch a wave together and call the three beats: paddle, pop, ride low.',
  play:'Cushion Waves: a wobbly cushion is the board, the carpet is the sea. Paddle on the tummy three reaches, pop up, hold the low stance three counts — RIDE! Then wobble the cushion gently with a toe while the surfer stays soft-kneed. Every fall is a wipeout, every wipeout is a laugh, every pop-up is the whole sport.',
  tips:'Do the pop-ups on carpet in socks — slides are the point, slips are the giggle. And teach the ocean\u2019s golden rule early and forever: waves are stronger than everyone; we swim and surf only where the watching grown-up says.',
  check:'Ask: "What are the three beats — paddle, and then? Are the knees soft or stiff? What stops the board flying away?" Pop up and ride, soft, the leash — three answers and the wave is caught.'},
 rowing:{
  title:'The Rowing Lesson',one:'Dip, pull, glide — the team sport where everyone faces backwards and moves as one.',
  seoTitle:'Rowing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Pull Rhythm and Rowing Together (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the rowing stroke — lean, pull, glide — with towel-pulling games that build pulling strength and crew rhythm.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches rowing\u2019s three-beat stroke and its deeper lesson: the boat only moves when EVERYBODY moves — in time, all together, one crew.',
  what:'Rowing is propelling a long thin boat with oars: legs push, body leans back, arms pull the water past — dip, pull, glide — over and over, facing backwards, in perfect time with the crew.',
  mean:'Rowing is the wall\u2019s one pulling sport: nearly everything else throws and pushes. And its rhythm is its rule — out-of-time strokes chop the boat, in-time strokes make it fly. Togetherness is not a bonus skill here; it is the engine.',
  know:'Your child needs three rowing ideas: the stroke has three beats — lean forward, pull back, glide; the legs start the power and the arms finish it; and the whole crew rows IN TIME — that is why rowers count out loud.',
  why:'Pulling strength is the rarest skill on this wall, and crew rhythm is the purest teamwork game there is — two gifts in one boat, learnable on a carpet with a towel.',
  dev:'Pulling strength (rows are the mirror of every push), sequenced power — legs, body, arms — and synchronised rhythm with a partner, the earliest real crew experience.',
  vocab:'rowing, oar, stroke, pull, crew, rhythm, glide',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is rowing — one boat, one crew, all together."\n2) The three beats: sit tall, LEAN forward, PULL back, GLIDE. Say it in a whisper-rhythm: lean… pull… glide…\n3) Leg power first: the push starts in the legs, the arms finish — legs, body, arms, in that order. Practise the order slowly.\n4) Backwards view: rowers face where they CAME from — ask why. (Because the pull is easier backwards! And the cox faces forward to steer.)\n5) Play the crew game below and close: "lean, pull, glide — all together."',
  say:'"Rowing is the together game in a long thin boat! Lean forward, pull the oars back, and glide — lean, pull, glide. The legs push first, the arms finish, and everybody does it AT THE SAME TIME — that is why rowers count out loud: one, two, pull! When the crew is in time, the boat flies. When it is not — splashy chaos!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "rowing" leads at the top in big letters with the water\u2019s scene below — the long boat, the oars and the crew exactly as the race looks from the bank.',
  ask:'"What are the three beats of the stroke?" "Which way does a rower face?" "What happens if the crew rows out of time?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Lean, pull, glide!" "Rowers sit backwards — funny!" or the crew call: "All together — NOW!"',
  real:'Rowing lives on rivers in every school town — Oxford and Cambridge race it, Boston and Melbourne row it. Watch one eight glide past together and listen for the count: the boat has a heartbeat and it is the crew\u2019s count.',
  play:'Crew Towel Row: sit on the floor facing a partner, both holding one towel or stick between you. Row in time: both lean forward, both pull back, counting "lean… pull… glide" — twenty strokes without dropping the rhythm. Then tug-of-water: gentle pulls against each other\u2019s hold, feel the pull in the arms. In-time beats strong — that is the crew\u2019s law.',
  tips:'Count EVERYTHING — the counting is the rhythm, and the rhythm is the skill. And keep pulls gentle in the towel game: this is rhythm training with strength sprinkled on top, not the other way round.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the three beats. Where does the power start? What must the whole crew share?" Lean-pull-glide, the legs, the rhythm — three answers and the boat flies.'},
 canoeing:{
  title:'The Canoeing Lesson',one:'One paddle, calm water, left and right — the quiet way to steer a boat.',
  seoTitle:'Canoeing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Paddle Sides and the Steering Secret (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the canoe paddle stroke, the left-right rule and the one-side steering trick, with rug-boat paddling games for young paddlers.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches canoeing\u2019s quiet physics: dip on the left, dip on the right, and the boat goes straight — paddle one side only, and the boat spins. Steering, discovered by playing.',
  what:'Canoeing is paddling an open boat with a single-bladed paddle, usually kneeling: dip, pull, switch sides, and the canoe glides ahead on calm water.',
  mean:'The canoe is a physics lesson in disguise: every paddle stroke turns the boat a little, so going straight means SHARING the strokes between sides. One side only spins you in a circle — and four-year-olds adore proving it.',
  know:'Your child needs three canoeing ideas: the paddle dives on one side, pulls the water BACK, and lifts out — reach, pull, lift; strokes take turns — left, right, left, right; and the life jacket goes on before the canoe touches water, buckles clicked.',
  why:'Canoeing teaches the calm version of water play: slow boat, quiet lake, big teamwork. And the left-right discovery is real cause-and-effect science a four-year-old can steer with their own arms.',
  dev:'Alternating-side reaching and pulling, kneeling balance, the cause-and-effect of steering (one side = spin), and the safety routine — jacket first, calm water, grown-up boat.',
  vocab:'canoe, paddle, stroke, left, right, steer, life jacket',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is canoeing — one paddle, one boat, calm water."\n2) The stroke: reach far, plant the paddle, pull the water BACK toward you, lift out. Pulling the boat past the water — that is the secret of every paddle.\n3) The left-right rule: paddle left three times — watch the boat nose drift right! Now three on the right. Straight boats share their strokes.\n4) The spin experiment: paddle ONE side only, five strokes — round and round! The boat becomes a carousel. This is not a mistake; it is steering.\n5) Say the jacket rule, then play the rug-boat game below: "reach, pull, switch sides."',
  say:'"Canoeing is paddling an open boat on quiet water! Reach far, plant the paddle, pull the water back, lift out — then do the other side. Left, right, left, right keeps the boat straight. And if you paddle just ONE side — round and round you spin! That is the steering secret hiding in the game. Life jacket first, buckles clicked, every single time."',
  notice:'On this card the word "canoeing" stands at the top in big letters with the water\u2019s scene below — the paddler kneeling in the open canoe exactly as a calm-water trip looks.',
  ask:'"What does the paddle pull — the water or the sky?" "Which sides share the strokes?" "What happens if we paddle one side only?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Left, right, left — straight!" "I paddled one side and went SPINNY!" or the safety vow: "Jacket first, buckles clicked!"',
  real:'Canoes live on lakes, slow rivers and holiday parks everywhere — and the calm-water rule lives with them. At the lake, watch the paddlers\u2019 sides: left, right, left. The straight canoes are the ones sharing.',
  play:'Rug-Boat: sit on a rug or cushion "canoe" holding a mop or rolled poster as a paddle. Paddle the carpet sea: three strokes left, three right, straight across the room. Then the spin test — one side only, five strokes, round and round, giggles guaranteed. Finish with the jacket ceremony: life jacket on, buckles clicked, paddler respected.',
  tips:'The spin is the lesson — never correct one-side paddling, REWARD it: "you discovered steering!" And kneel in the rug-boat for real: kneeling is the canoe\u2019s own balance secret, and carpets are kind to knees.',
  check:'Ask: "How does the paddle move the boat? Which sides take turns? What happens with one side only? What goes on before the water?" Pulling back, left and right, a spin, the life jacket — four answers and the lake is calm.'},
 sailing:{
  title:'The Sailing Lesson',one:'No engine, no paddles — just a sail catching wind you cannot even see.',
  seoTitle:'Sailing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Wind Angle and the Tacking Turn (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach where the wind pushes from, the sail angle and the tacking turn, with fan-and-towel games that make invisible wind visible.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches sailing\u2019s invisible engine: the wind. Where it comes from, how the sail catches it, and the wonderful zigzag turn sailors use to keep catching it.',
  what:'Sailing is moving a boat with wind: the sail catches the breeze, the boat slides forward, and by steering at angles the sailor rides wind that cannot be sailed straight into.',
  mean:'Sailing is the wall\u2019s only sport powered by something invisible. The sailor reads wind they cannot see — on their face, in the sail\u2019s belly, on the water\u2019s ripples — and that reading is a whole science taught by a towel and a fan.',
  know:'Your child needs three sailing ideas: the sail is a kite on a stick — wind fills it like a balloon and the boat slides; you cannot sail STRAIGHT into wind — sail must zigzag at angles (tacking); and the life jacket goes on first, because the wind does not ask permission.',
  why:'The fan-and-sail game turns wind — invisible, abstract — into a toy the child can aim and feel. It is physics, weather and sport in one afternoon, and it ends with a zigzag race across the living room.',
  dev:'Sensing an invisible force (wind on face, sail fill), angle thinking — the zigzag path — cause-and-effect steering, and the jacket-first water ritual.',
  vocab:'sailing, wind, sail, catch, tack, zigzag, life jacket',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is sailing — the wind pushes, the boat glides."\n2) Feel the wind: blow on the back of the hand, fan the face — wind is moving air, and moving air PUSHES.\n3) The sail test: hold a towel or pillow up like a sail and walk — nothing. Now a partner FANS it — the sail fills, the "boat" wants to slide. Wind caught!\n4) The zigzag rule: try to walk straight INTO the fan — the sail flaps dead. Step sideways at an angle — full power! Sailors zigzag because wind only pushes from the side.\n5) Say the jacket rule, then play the fan-sail race below: "catch the wind, tack, glide."',
  say:'"Sailing has no engine and no paddles — the WIND is the engine! The sail catches it like a kite, and the boat slides across the water. But here is the secret: wind will not push a boat head-on. Sailors zigzag — one angle, then the other, tacking back and forth all the way to where they want to go. And life jacket first, always — the wind does not ask permission."',
  notice:'On this card the word "sailing" stands big at the top with the water\u2019s scene below — the small boat and the full white sail exactly as a breeze-day on the bay looks.',
  ask:'"What pushes a sailing boat?" "Can we sail straight into the wind?" "What do sailors do instead?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The wind is the engine!" "I caught the wind in my sail!" or the sailor\u2019s plan: "Zigzag — tack tack tack!"',
  real:'Sailing lives at every yacht club, harbour and breezy lake — and kids\u2019 sailing courses start with exactly this: feel the wind, fill the sail, tack. On windy walks, find the flags and ripples together: the wind is writing its address all over the water.',
  play:'Fan-Sail Race: one player is the WIND with a paper fan; the other is the BOAT holding a towel-sail. The wind fan-blasts from different spots; the boat must keep the sail angled to catch it — never head-on! — and zigzag from sofa to sofa. Swap roles. The invisible force becomes a game piece, and tacking becomes the family\u2019s new favourite word.',
  tips:'Let the head-on failure HAPPEN — the flapping dead sail is the moment the angle rule makes sense; no diagram teaches it faster. And on real water days, the jacket rule is said out loud every single time, without exceptions to negotiate.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the engine of a sailboat? Why do sailors zigzag? What goes on first?" The wind, wind will not push head-on, the life jacket — three answers and the bay is open.'},
 skating:{
  title:'The Skating Lesson',one:'Little steps, then the glide — the wobbliest, shiniest eight metres in sport.',
  seoTitle:'Skating Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Glide, the Wobble and Falling Soft (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the skating march, the first glide and the soft safe fall, with slipper-skating games for hallway ice rinks.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches skating where the ice is the hallway floor: the marching steps, the magical glide when the feet stop moving, and the soft fall every skater keeps in their pocket.',
  what:'Skating is gliding on blades across ice: little marching steps first, then standing still while the ice carries you — the glide — then pushes and swoops and eventually spins, all on the shiniest, slipperiest floor there is.',
  mean:'Skating flips walking\u2019s rules: the floor MOVES. The skill is trusting the glide — feet still, body tall, momentum doing the work. Every skater remembers their first true glide the way other children remember their first bike.',
  know:'Your child needs three skating ideas: the march is small steps with toes turned slightly out; the glide is feet parallel, knees soft, NO stepping — let the ice (or floor) carry you; and falling is a SKILL: knees down, hands on knees, soft and giggly, always planned never feared.',
  why:'Falling safely might be the most reusable skill in this whole class — skaters, judoka and playground kids all borrow it. And slipper-skating on the hallway floor builds the glide with zero cold, zero ice and zero bruises.',
  dev:'Dynamic balance on a low-friction surface, the stillness-while-moving glide, planned falling (a confidence skill as much as a safety one), and small-step bravery on new ground.',
  vocab:'skating, ice, glide, march, wobble, fall soft, blades',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is skating — gliding on the shiny ice."\n2) The march: little steps, toes out slightly, arms out like aeroplane wings. March a hallway lap.\n3) The glide: feet together-ish, knees soft, and STOP stepping — let the floor carry you. Count the glide: one… two… three! Every glide longer than the last.\n4) The soft fall: practise ON PURPOSE — sink to the knees, hands on knees, giggle. "Every skater falls; good skaters fall soft." Do it three times so it is a friend, not a fear.\n5) Play the slipper-rink game below and close: "march, glide, fall soft."',
  say:'"Skating is gliding on ice! Little marching steps first — toes out, wings out. Then the magic: stop stepping, knees soft, and LET THE ICE CARRY YOU. That glide is the best feeling in sport. And when we wobble — we fall SOFT: knees down, hands on knees, giggle, up again. Every skater falls; the good ones make friends with it."',
  notice:'On this card the word "skating" is printed large at the top with the rink\u2019s scene below — the skater on shining blades exactly as a lap of the ice looks.',
  ask:'"What do our feet do first — march or glide?" "What do the knees do in a glide?" "What do we practise so falling is not scary?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "I glided three whole counts!" "Wings out, toes out!" or the proud skater\u2019s vow: "I fall soft — knees down, giggle!"',
  real:'Skating lives at every ice rink and winter festival — and in socks on every polished hallway floor. At the rink, hunt for glides: which skaters step-step-step forever, and which ones stand still and FLY?',
  play:'Slipper-Rink: socks on a smooth floor, the hallway is the rink. March the length, then glide — count it out loud. Add "hoops" (taped squares) to glide THROUGH, and the planned soft fall on a rug at the end, giggling. Helmet on if the floor is extra slippery — real rinks say yes to helmets for beginners, always.',
  tips:'Play glide contests, not speed races — stillness is the skill and counting the glide makes it visible. And do the deliberate soft fall until it is BORING: a fall that is boring is a fall that never scares.',
  check:'Ask: "What comes first, march or glide? What do the knees do? What do we practise on purpose?" March, soft, the soft fall — three answers and the hallway is an ice rink.'},
 skiing:{
  title:'The Skiing Lesson',one:'Pizza to slow, french fries to go — the mountain\u2019s two shapes and one brave hill.',
  seoTitle:'Skiing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Snowplough Stop and Mountain Knees (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the pizza wedge stop, the french-fries glide and soft mountain knees, with carpet-slope games before the real snow.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches skiing through its famous lunch menu: tips together for pizza (slow and stopped), tips apart for french fries (fast and free) — the two shapes every skier on Earth learns first.',
  what:'Skiing is sliding down snow on long boards: legs bouncing like springs, tips together in a wedge (pizza!) to slow down, tips parallel (french fries!) to glide, poles trailing behind — the mountain does the pushing, the skier does the steering.',
  mean:'Skiing inverts the usual rule: here the STOP is learned before the speed. The pizza wedge gives a four-year-old a brake they control with their own legs — which is why ski schools worldwide teach it before anything else.',
  know:'Your child needs three skiing ideas: pizza — ski tips together, heels out, the wedge that slows and stops; french fries — boards parallel, the glide that goes; and mountain knees are SOFT knees — the springs that drink every bump.',
  why:'Named-after-lunch skills are unforgettable, and the wedge is safety and skill in one shape. Practised in socks down a carpet slope, it hands the child their brake before the mountain ever does.',
  dev:'Wedge braking from the hips, soft-knee absorption (springs, not sticks), edge awareness, and the mountain\u2019s order of operations: stop first, speed second, always.',
  vocab:'skiing, snow, pizza, french fries, wedge, glide, slope',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is skiing — sliding down the mountain on long boards."\n2) Pizza: heels slide OUT, tips together — a thin wedge. Hold the shape. That is the brake.\n3) French fries: boards slide parallel — the glide. Say both shapes, be both shapes.\n4) Mountain knees: bounce gently — soft springs. Stiff legs bump, soft legs drink the bumps. Bounce-test everything.\n5) Play carpet-slope below and close: "pizza to stop, fries to go, springs all the way."',
  say:'"Skiing is sliding down the snow! Want to slow down? Tips together — PIZZA! The wedge stops the whole mountain. Want to glide? Boards straight — FRENCH FRIES! And your knees stay soft like springs the whole time, drinking every bump. Stop first, speed second — that is the mountain\u2019s rule, and lunch names the moves."',
  notice:'On this card the word "skiing" leads at the top in big letters with the snow\u2019s scene below — the skier on the snowy slope exactly as a green-run morning looks.',
  ask:'"Which shape slows us down?" "Which shape lets us glide?" "What are mountain knees — sticks or springs?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Pizza! Tips together!" "Fries — I\u2019m going fast!" or the mountain law: "Stop first, speed second!"',
  real:'Skiing owns the winter holidays of the US Rockies, the Canadian powder, the Australian and NZ snow fields — and every ski school on Earth teaches pizza first. On a snowy screen, hunt for the wedge: every beginner is a pizza; every racer is a fry.',
  play:'Carpet Slope: socks on, a smooth rug as the "run," the hallway the "mountain." Side-step UP the slope (skiers sidestep!), then slide down — fries for three counts, PIZZA to stop before the cushion-trees. Ten runs, alternating shapes. Then the bounce test: stiff legs vs spring legs over a bumpy towel — springs win, always.',
  tips:'Practise the wedge STANDING STILL until the heels know the slide-out — then take it downhill. And keep the run short and the trees (cushions) far apart: early slopes are for shape-building, not courage-testing.',
  check:'Ask: "Which shape is the brake? Which shape is the glide? What do mountain knees drink?" Pizza, french fries, the bumps — three answers and the mountain is ready.'},
 curling:{
  title:'The Curling Lesson',one:'Slide the stone, sweep the ice — the gentle game where friends steer your throw after you let go.',
  seoTitle:'Curling Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Slide, the Aim and the Sweep (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the curling slide, the target aim and the sweeping game, with kitchen-floor stones and paper-plate houses.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches curling\u2019s friendly surprise: you slide the stone and then your TEAMMATES sweep the ice to steer it — the only sport where your throw keeps changing after your hand lets go.',
  what:'Curling is a target game on ice: slide a big polished stone down the sheet toward a target of rings (the house), while teammates sweep the ice in front of it to make it go farther or curl less.',
  mean:'Curling splits a throw into two jobs — the slider aims, the sweepers power. No other sport hands your throw to your friends mid-flight, which makes curling the gentlest team game on the wall.',
  know:'Your child needs three curling ideas: the stone is SLID, low and smooth, never thrown; the house is the target — rings to land in, closest to the middle wins; and sweeping melts a whisper of ice so the stone runs farther — sweeping is real, not pretend.',
  why:'Kitchen-floor curling needs nothing but a lid and a paper plate, and the physics is real: smooth floor, sliding object, friction — the whole sport is a science experiment with a broom.',
  dev:'Underhand sliding with force control, target aiming, cooperative sweeping (helping a moving thing WITHOUT touching it), and turn order — curling is famously polite.',
  vocab:'curling, stone, slide, sweep, house, target, ice',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is curling — sliding the stone to the rings on the ice."\n2) The slide: crouch low, push the stone (a lid, a bean bag) along the floor with a smooth underhand slide — long and low, like feeding a cat under a fence. No throws!\n3) The house: paper plates in rings — middle is the button, landing near the middle wins.\n4) The sweep: two teammates fan or tea-towel the floor IN FRONT of the moving stone — never touching it. Watch: swept floors let stones run farther. Real physics!\n5) Play the kitchen curling game below and close: "slide low, aim middle, sweep sweep sweep."',
  say:'"Curling is sliding a big smooth stone across the ice toward the rings — the house! Slide it LOW, never throw it. And here is the magic: your friends sweep the ice in front of the stone — sweep sweep sweep — and the sweeping makes it go FARTHER. You aim it, your friends steer it. The politest game on ice."',
  notice:'On this card the word "curling" stands big at the top with the rink\u2019s scene below — the sliding stone and the target rings exactly as the game looks on the sheet.',
  ask:'"Do we throw the stone or slide it?" "What is the target called?" "What do the sweepers\u2019 brushes do to the ice?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "I slid it to the button!" "Sweep sweep — it went farther!" or the curler\u2019s shout: "HURRY HARD!"',
  real:'Curling lives in Canada\u2019s rinks and winter Olympics screens everywhere — and its soul lives on kitchen floors. Watch one end of a real game together: the slide, the sweepers, the polite nod. The politest athletes in the world.',
  play:'Kitchen Curling: paper-plate rings at one end of the smooth floor, plastic-lid stones, tea-towel brushes. Slide for the button — five stones each, closest wins the end. The sweeping rule: brushes may fan the floor in front of a moving stone, never touch it. Then try the spin: a little wrist twist makes the stone CURL — curve into the house like a proper curler.',
  tips:'Smooth floors only — rugs are the ice\u2019s enemy. And let the sweepers truly matter: their fan-power genuinely carries stones farther, which turns teammates into co-throwers and keeps every player in the game.',
  check:'Ask: "Slide or throw? What is the target called? What does sweeping do?" Slide, the house, makes it go farther — three answers and the kitchen is a rink.'},
 skateboarding:{
  title:'The Skateboarding Lesson',one:'Stand sideways, push, roll — the board that turns pavement into a playground.',
  seoTitle:'Skateboarding Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Stance, the Push and the Safety Gear (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the sideways stance, the gentle push and the helmet-and-pads ritual, with carpet-stance games before the first roll.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches skateboarding where it always starts: the stance on carpet — feet sideways, knees soft — because the strangest thing about a skateboard is that your body faces where the board is not going.',
  what:'Skateboarding is riding a board on four small wheels: one foot forward near the front, push with the back foot, stand sideways, knees soft, and roll — flat ground first, ramps much later.',
  mean:'Every other board on this wall points where you look. The skateboard stands you SIDEWAYS — and until the body accepts that, nothing else happens. The carpet stance solves the strangeness before the wheels exist.',
  know:'Your child needs three skateboarding ideas: the stance is sideways — front foot near the bolts, body facing the side; the push is gentle back-foot scoots, then that foot joins the board; and the gear rule is helmet AND pads before the board ever rolls.',
  why:'The carpet-stance game removes skateboarding\u2019s only scary part — facing sideways on wheels — and the helmet-and-pads ritual, worn from the first roll, is the habit that keeps skaters rolling for decades.',
  dev:'Side-on balance (new for every board game before it), single-foot pushing while the other balances, gear rituals, and the patience of flat ground before ramps.',
  vocab:'skateboard, stance, sideways, push, roll, helmet, pads',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is skateboarding — standing sideways on a rolling board."\n2) Carpet stance: board (or cushion) on carpet so it cannot roll. Feet SIDEWAYS over the bolts, knees soft, arms out. Hold five counts. Swap which foot leads — both are fine.\n3) The push: back foot off, little scoots along the carpet, foot back on. Scoot, scoot, ride.\n4) The gear ceremony: helmet on and buckled, pads on knees and elbows — the ritual that goes on BEFORE the wheels, every time, no exceptions.\n5) First roll (when ready): smooth flat pavement, gentle scoots, roll three metres, stamp the board still. Close with: "sideways, soft knees, gear on."',
  say:'"Skateboarding is standing SIDEWAYS on a rolling board! Feet over the bolts, knees soft, arms out — the board goes forward but YOU face the side, and that is the fun of it. Little scoots with the back foot, then roll. And the rule that never bends: helmet buckled and pads on BEFORE the wheels move. Sideways, soft knees, gear on — then we roll."',
  notice:'On this card the word "skateboarding" is printed large at the top with the street\u2019s scene below — the rider balanced on the wheeled board exactly as a first roll looks.',
  ask:'"Which way do our shoulders face — forward or sideways?" "Which foot pushes?" "What goes on before the wheels roll?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "I stand sideways — the board faces forward!" "I pushed and rolled!" or the gear vow: "Helmet and pads first!"',
  real:'Skateboarding lives on every driveway, skatepark and school yard — and its Olympic debut made it official. Watch one park run together and find the soft knees: every smooth trick starts with bent springs, not stiff sticks.',
  play:'Carpet Stance School: board on carpet — sideways hold, soft knees, five counts, swap lead foot. Scoot laps along the carpet, push foot silent like a secret agent. Then the gear ceremony and (on a calm day) the first real three-metre roll on flat pavement, stamped to a stop. The stance was learned where nothing could roll — the street just receives it.',
  tips:'Never skip the gear ceremony, not even for one push — the ritual IS the lesson, and rituals kept at four keep riders rolling at fourteen. And carpet first, always: a board that cannot roll lets the body learn the sideways truth safely.',
  check:'Ask: "Which way do we stand? What do the knees stay? What goes on before the wheels?" Sideways, soft, helmet and pads — three answers and the pavement is a playground.'},
 climbing:{
  title:'The Climbing Lesson',one:'Grip, reach, plan — the thinking sport that goes straight up.',
  seoTitle:'Climbing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Grip, Reach and the Three-Move Plan (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the climbing grip, the cross-body reach and the plan-ahead habit, with wall-tape and playground games for small climbers.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches climbing as the thinking sport: the next hold is a question, the body answers with grip and reach, and the best climbers plan three moves before the first one.',
  what:'Climbing is going UP a wall using hands and feet: grip the holds, push with the legs, reach for the next one — and always climb down as carefully as up. On playground ladders or real gym walls, the rules are the same.',
  mean:'Climbing is cross-body thinking: right hand with left foot, reach across the middle, plan before moving. It builds grip strength, sequencing and the calm kind of bravery — and the descent is trained as hard as the climb.',
  know:'Your child needs three climbing ideas: the legs PUSH more than the arms pull — tired climbers find legs again; the reach crosses the body — right hand to a left-ish hold makes an X; and the rule of every wall: come down SLOW, three points touching when you can.',
  why:'Grip strength and cross-lateral planning are two of the most borrowable skills on this wall — climbers grow into every racquet, bat and bar. And a child who plans three moves ahead has learned thinking-as-sport.',
  dev:'Grip strength and endurance, cross-lateral reaching, move sequencing (plan, then do), and the calm-down-skill — descending carefully is the deepest bravery in the lesson.',
  vocab:'climbing, grip, reach, hold, plan, down, brave',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is climbing — going up the wall with hands and feet planning together."\n2) The grip: fingers and thumbs wrap the hold like holding a big jar lid — whole hand, not just fingertips. Squeeze-test a doorway.\n3) The X-reach: practise on the floor first — right hand reaches up-LEFT, left foot steps — the body makes a letter X. Climbers are crossed.\n4) The plan: point at three holds and say them — red, blue, high one — THEN climb. Planning before moving is the sport\u2019s brain.\n5) Play the tape-wall game below, and say the down rule: "up slow, down slower."',
  say:'"Climbing is the thinking sport! Grip the hold like a jar lid, push with your strong legs, and reach ACROSS your body — right hand to the left-ish hold, an X! And before we climb, we PLAN: point at three holds, say their names, then go. And the rule that makes climbers brave: up slow, down SLOWER. Coming down is part of the climb."',
  notice:'On this card the word "climbing" leads at the top in big letters with the wall\u2019s scene below — the climber on the colourful holds exactly as a gym wall looks from the mats.',
  ask:'"What pushes more — arms or legs?" "Which way does a climbing reach go — straight up or across in an X?" "What do we plan before we climb?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Legs are the strong ones!" "I planned three holds!" or the climber\u2019s rule: "Up slow, down slower!"',
  real:'Climbing lives at every gym wall, playground ladder and climbing frame — and kids\u2019 climbing courses teach exactly this: grip, X-reach, plan, down slow. On the playground, watch a climber\u2019s feet: the strong ones push, they never dangle.',
  play:'Tape-Wall Climbing: paper "holds" taped at reaching heights on a smooth wall — feet stay on the floor. Touch the holds in planned order: call three, climb the pattern, hands only. Then the playground version: the ladder or frame with the three-point rule and the down-slower rule. Grip grows, the X grows, and the plan comes before every move.',
  tips:'Feet-on-floor tape climbing is a real climbing workout — grip and reach grow without any height at all. And praise the careful DESCENT louder than the top-out: children who are celebrated for down-climbing stay safe on every wall they will ever meet.',
  check:'Ask: "What pushes more? What letter does the reach make? What do we plan? And how do we come down?" The legs, an X, three holds, slower — four answers and the wall is friendly.'},
 'horse-riding':{
  title:'The Horse Riding Lesson',one:'Sit tall, heels down, gentle hands — the sport where your teammate is a pony with opinions.',
  seoTitle:'Horse Riding Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Rider Seat, Soft Hands and Helmet Rule (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the tall rider seat, the soft-rein hands and the helmet-first rule, with cushion-pony games before the riding school.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches riding before any pony arrives: the tall seat, the soft hands, the walking-pace trot rhythm — so the first real ride feels like meeting a friend who already knows the rules.',
  what:'Horse riding is riding with a large, kind animal: sit tall on the saddle, heels down, hold the reins gently, and ask with your seat and voice — walk, trot, whoa. The pony is a teammate, not a machine.',
  mean:'Riding is the wall\u2019s only partnership sport — half the team weighs half a tonne and has its own opinions. Children learn that calm voices, soft hands and patience move ponies; yanking and squeaking do not. It is manners with hooves.',
  know:'Your child needs three riding ideas: the seat is TALL — sit up like a flame on a candle, heels down; the hands are SOFT — hold the reins like holding a baby bird; and the helmet goes on FIRST, buckled, every ride, every pony, no exceptions.',
  why:'Beyond the joy, riding teaches emotional regulation in a way no ball game can: the pony reads your body. A calm rider IS a safe rider — and that skill walks off the yard into the rest of life.',
  dev:'Posture and tall-seated balance, soft-grip hands (fine motor with a live partner), rhythm following in the rising trot, and the calm-body skill that ponies demand and reward.',
  vocab:'horse riding, pony, saddle, reins, helmet, trot, calm',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is horse riding — sitting tall on a pony teammate."\n2) The seat: sit on a chair or cushion-horse — tall as a flame, shoulders back, HEELS DOWN. That is the rider\u2019s shape.\n3) The soft hands: hold a rope or ribbon as reins — soft fists, arms elastic. Yank the rein? The pony\u2019s mouth hurts. Soft hands all the way.\n4) The rising trot: up-down, up-down — stand and sit in rhythm while a partner counts or claps. That beat is the trot.\n5) The helmet ceremony: helmet ON, buckled, before any pony is mentioned. Then play the cushion-pony school below: "tall seat, soft hands, helmet first."',
  say:'"Horse riding is sitting tall on a pony teammate! Back straight like a flame on a candle, heels down, and hold the reins SOFT — like holding a baby bird. Your voice asks: walk on! And your seat says whoa. The pony listens to calm bodies. And the rule before everything, every ride: helmet on, buckled up. Tall seat, soft hands, helmet first."',
  notice:'On this card the words "horse riding" stand at the top in big letters with the yard\u2019s scene below — the rider in a helmet on the pony exactly as a riding-school lesson looks.',
  ask:'"How do we sit — slumped or tall as a flame?" "How do hands hold the reins — tight or soft as a baby bird?" "What goes on our head first?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Heels down, sit tall!" "Soft hands — the pony likes gentle!" or the vow: "Helmet first, every ride!"',
  real:'Riding schools everywhere teach exactly this first: helmet, tall seat, soft hands, calm voice. Near a paddock, watch the riders\u2019 hands — the ponies of soft-handed riders walk relaxed. The partnership is visible.',
  play:'Cushion-Pony School: a cushion on a chair is the pony. Mount from the "block" (step), find the tall seat, heels down, ribbon-reins in soft fists. The grown-up is the pony: walk, turn, halt — the rider asks with voice and seat, and the pony ONLY listens to soft hands and a calm voice. Rising trot to a clap rhythm. End with dismount and the helmet unbuckled — the ceremony closes like it opened.',
  tips:'Never rush the real pony — the cushion school carries weeks of prep, and children who arrive knowing the seat and the soft hands are the ones riding schools remember fondly. And the helmet rule has no clever exceptions: it is said every lesson, out loud, first.',
  check:'Ask: "How does a rider sit? How do hands hold the reins? What comes first, every ride?" Tall as a flame, soft as a baby bird, the helmet — three answers and the yard is ready.'},
 archery:{
  title:'The Archery Lesson',one:'Stand side-on, draw slow, let go soft — the quietest aiming sport there is.',
  seoTitle:'Archery Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Side-on Stance and the Slow Release (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the archery stance, the slow draw and the calm release, with suction-cup and rubber-band games for steady young aimers.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches archery\u2019s secret: the arrow goes where the CALM goes. The stance, the slow draw and the gentle release are all just calm — practised until it aims.',
  what:'Archery is shooting arrows at a target with a bow: stand side-on, draw the string back slow, hold one quiet breath, and let go soft. The target\u2019s middle — the gold — is the dream, but the routine is the skill.',
  mean:'Every sport on this wall rewards speed somewhere. Archery is the one that rewards STILLNESS — the slow draw and the held breath are self-regulation dressed as sport, which is why calm children discover they were archers all along.',
  know:'Your child needs three archery ideas: the stance is side-on — feet apart, body facing the target\u2019s edge; the draw is SLOW — string to the cheek, one breath held; and the release is a soft finger-open, never a fling. Plus the range rule, always: arrows up only when everyone is behind the line.',
  why:'A suction-cup bow makes the sport safe, and the routine makes it teachable: stand, draw, breathe, release — a four-beat calm that children carry into every other waiting moment of their day.',
  dev:'Side-on stance, slow controlled pulling (draw strength is gentle strength), breath control and stillness, and the range discipline of lines and turn-taking — safety as a shared ritual.',
  vocab:'archery, bow, arrow, draw, aim, release, target',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is archery — the quiet aiming game with a bow and arrow."\n2) The stance: side-on, feet apart, body facing the target\u2019s shoulder — like a tennis serve\u2019s sideways cousin.\n3) The draw: string back SLOW, hand to the cheek — the anchor. Hold one breath. The holding IS the aiming.\n4) The release: fingers open SOFT — the arrow is let go, never thrown. Practise the soft-open ten times with no arrow.\n5) Say the range rule, then play the target game below: "side-on, draw slow, let go soft."',
  say:'"Archery is the quiet game! Stand side-on, draw the string back slow — to your cheek, hold one breath — and let go SOFT with open fingers. The arrow flies where the calm goes. And the range rule that never bends: arrows up only when everyone is behind the line. Side-on, draw slow, let go soft — bullseye or not, the calm is the prize."',
  notice:'On this card the word "archery" stands large at the top with the range\u2019s scene below — the bow, the arrow and the round target exactly as a shooting line looks.',
  ask:'"Which way do we stand — facing or side-on?" "Fast draw or slow draw?" "What does the release look like — a fling or a soft open?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Draw slow… hold… let go soft!" "My arrow flew straight!" or the range rule: "Arrows up when everyone\u2019s behind the line!"',
  real:'Archery lives at clubs, camps and school sport days — suction-cup bows for beginners everywhere. On Olympic screens, watch one archer\u2019s face at full draw: calm as a lake. That calm is the whole sport, visible.',
  play:'Paper-Plate Range: paper-plate target on a chair at one end of the room, suction-cup bow (or rubber-band cork-bow). The four-beat routine every shot: side-on… draw slow… one breath… soft open. Five arrows each, score the rings, praise the CALMEST shot loudest — not the closest. The range closes the same way it opened: arrows down, everyone behind the line.',
  tips:'Praise routine over hits for the first month — a calm four-beat shot that misses teaches more than a fluke bullseye. And the line rule is a game, not a lecture: the "range officer" (rotating role) calls "arrows up!" — children love enforcing calm.',
  check:'Ask: "How do we stand? How do we draw? How do we let go? Who says when arrows can go up?" Side-on, slow, soft open, the range officer — four answers and the range is calm.'},
 bowling:{
  title:'The Bowling Lesson',one:'Roll it low, count the pins — the birthday-party sport hiding a maths lesson.',
  seoTitle:'Bowling Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Floor Roll and Pin Counting (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the underhand floor roll, the straight aim and the pin counting, with water-bottle lanes for rainy-day active play.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches bowling\u2019s one golden rule — ROLL, never throw — and its quiet second life as the counting game: every fallen pin is a number shouted with joy.',
  what:'Bowling is rolling a ball down a wooden lane to knock over ten white pins: one hand underneath, a smooth floor-level roll, and then the count — how many fell, how many stand?',
  mean:'The underneath-roll is a genuine gross motor skill almost no other sport teaches — a push-along-the-ground that wants NO air time. And the scoring is the friendliest maths in sport: counting that everyone cheers.',
  know:'Your child needs three bowling ideas: the ball ROLLS — one hand underneath, low and smooth along the floor, never thrown; the aim is the arrow marks or just the middle pin; and every roll ends in a count — fallen pins first, standing pins second.',
  why:'Bowling is the perfect rainy-day card: a hallway, six bottles and any ball become a whole bowling alley — and the counting makes it a maths lesson wearing crash helmets.',
  dev:'Underhand ground-level rolling with force control, straight-line aiming, and the count — one-to-ten maths with crash sound effects built in.',
  vocab:'bowling, roll, pins, lane, strike, count, aim',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is bowling — rolling the ball at the pins."\n2) The roll: one hand UNDER the ball, swing it back low, let it roll OFF the fingers along the floor — like bowling it under a table. Practise the low swing five times, no ball.\n3) The aim: pick the middle pin and roll at IT — a small target makes straight rolls happen.\n4) The count: CRASH! — then count the fallen pins together, then the standing ones. Fallen plus standing should equal the whole set — that is the maths magic.\n5) Play the bottle-lane game below and close: "roll low, aim middle, count them all."',
  say:'"Bowling is the rolling game! One hand underneath, swing it back low, and ROLL it along the floor — never throw! Aim at the middle pin… roll… CRASH! Now count with me: how many fell down? How many still stand? Fallen plus standing makes ALL the pins — bowling is maths with crash sounds!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "bowling" leads at the top in big letters with the lane\u2019s scene below — the rolling ball and the standing pins exactly as the approach looks.',
  ask:'"Do we throw or roll?" "Which pin do we aim at?" "What do we do after the crash?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Roll low — under the table!" "I knocked down five!" or the bowling maths: "Four fell, six stand — ten in all!"',
  real:'Bowling lives at every bowling alley and birthday party — bumper lanes and light balls exist exactly for small rollers. At the alley, watch the approach: good rollers stay LOW. The floor is the ball\u2019s road, not the air\u2019s.',
  play:'Bottle-Lane: six water bottles at the hallway\u2019s end, any mid-size ball. Roll low from a tape line — five rolls each, count every crash, keep a shouting scoreboard. Then the maths magic: fallen plus standing always equals six. Rearrange the survivors into new shapes each round — triangles, towers — and roll again.',
  tips:'The under-the-table image fixes high throws instantly: "roll it like you\u2019re bowling it under a table" works where "roll gently" never does. And keep the lane smooth and the pins forgiving — empty bottles fall beautifully and stand up again without tears.',
  check:'Ask: "Throw or roll? Which pin is the target? What do we shout while counting?" Roll, the middle pin, the numbers — three answers and the lane is open.'},
 fencing:{
  title:'The Fencing Lesson',one:'En garde, advance, lunge — sword-fighting\u2019s polite cousin, all masks and footwork.',
  seoTitle:'Fencing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching En Garde, Footwork and the Pool-Noodle Duel (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the en-garde stance, the advance-and-retreat footwork and the cushion lunge, with pool-noodle duels for quick, polite fencers.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches fencing\u2019s real secret: it is a footwork sport wearing swords. The stance, the steps and the one big lunge carry the whole first lesson — the poking can wait a year.',
  what:'Fencing is one-on-one sword play with masks and thin bending blades: en garde stance, advance and retreat on the feet, and touch the opponent\u2019s jacket with the tip to score. It is chess at sprint speed, wrapped in extraordinary politeness.',
  mean:'Fencing\u2019s footwork is its treasure: side-on stance, quick small steps, balance held while moving fast — the same footwork boxers, tennis players and basketball defenders all borrow. The sword is almost a prop; the feet are the lesson.',
  know:'Your child needs three fencing ideas: en garde — side-on, knees bent, sword up, mask on; the feet do the fighting — advance forward, retreat back, small quick steps; and the lunge is ONE big step with the front foot and a long arm — then straight back to en garde.',
  why:'A pool-noodle duel makes the sport safe, and the etiquette makes it extraordinary: fencers salute before and shake hands after, every bout, forever. The manners are not decoration — they are the sport.',
  dev:'Side-on stance under movement, advance-retreat footwork (a rhythm skill), the lunge — one big coordinated step-and-reach — and the salute-and-handshake ritual.',
  vocab:'fencing, en garde, advance, retreat, lunge, mask, salute',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is fencing — the sword game with masks and manners."\n2) En garde: side-on, feet in a comfortable L, knees bent, noodle-sword UP, mask (or helmet-pretend) on. Hold the stance — it should feel springy.\n3) The footwork: ADVANCE — front foot steps, back foot follows. RETREAT — back foot steps, front follows. Small quick steps, up the hallway and back.\n4) The lunge: front foot BIG step, arm reaches long — touch the cushion-target — then straight back to en garde. One lunge is one whole firework.\n5) Play the noodle duel below and close with the salute: "en garde, advance, LUNGE — and always salute."',
  say:'"Fencing is the polite sword game! Mask on, en garde — side-on, knees springy, sword up. The feet do the fighting: advance… retreat… quick little steps. Then ONE big lunge — step and reach, touch the target — and back to en garde. And before every bout we salute, and after we shake hands. Fencers are the politest sword-fighters in the world."',
  notice:'On this card the word "fencing" stands at the top in big letters with the strip\u2019s scene below — two masked fencers and their crossed thin swords exactly as a bout looks.',
  ask:'"What do the feet do — big stomps or quick small steps?" "What is the one big step called?" "What do fencers do before and after every bout?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "En garde!" "Advance, advance, LUNGE!" or the fencer\u2019s manners: "We salute first — and shake hands after!"',
  real:'Fencing lives at clubs and school gyms — and on Olympic screens where the footwork is easiest to see. Watch one bout together and ignore the swords entirely: watch the FEET. Quick small steps, always springy.',
  play:'Noodle Duel: two pool-noodle swords, cushion-target between the fencers. Round one: footwork only — advance and retreat, mirror each other, no touching. Round two: one lunge each at the cushion, back to en garde. Round three: the mask-on duel — gentle noodle touches on the sword or target only, never people — then the salute and the handshake. Salute first, shake after: the ritual IS the sport.',
  tips:'Footwork rounds outnumber duelling rounds three to one — the feet are the curriculum. And noodle touches land on swords and targets ONLY; the rule is stated before round one and honoured by the grown-up\u2019s own noodle first.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the stance called? What do the feet do? What is the one big step? What happens before and after?" En garde, advance and retreat, the lunge, the salute — four answers and the strip is open.'},
 boxing:{
  title:'The Boxing Lesson',one:'Hands up, feet springy, jab the pads — the fitness sport where the rule IS the lesson.',
  seoTitle:'Boxing Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Guard, the Jab and the Pads-Only Rule (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the boxing guard, the straight jab and the pad-work round, with cushion-pad games where punching is allowed and targets are agreed.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches boxing the way good gyms do: stance and guard first, straight punches at PADS only, and the rule spoken out loud in every round — pads and bags, never people.',
  what:'Boxing is the punch sport with big gloves: guard up protecting the face, feet springy, straight jabs and crosses at pads, bags and nothing else. Real boxers are among the fittest athletes alive.',
  mean:'For little ones, boxing is pad-work fitness: a strong stance, fast straight arms, bouncing feet — and the discipline of WHERE punching is allowed. The rule is not a warning on the side of the lesson; the rule is the lesson.',
  know:'Your child needs three boxing ideas: the guard — fists up by the cheeks, elbows in, protecting the face; the jab is STRAIGHT — out and back down the same road, fast; and the rule — pads, bags and cushions are the only targets, ever, in every round.',
  why:'Pad rounds are pure joy — thirty seconds of jab-jab-cross with a roaring finish — and they build the strongest shoulders and straightest punches on this wall, inside the safest rule in sport.',
  dev:'Straight punching mechanics (out-and-back on one road), guard posture, springy footwork, thirty-second effort bursts, and rule-keeping under excitement — the deepest skill in the gym.',
  vocab:'boxing, guard, jab, cross, pads, round, straight',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is boxing — the pad-punching fitness sport."\n2) The guard: fists up by the cheeks, elbows tucked, chin soft. Say the rule BEFORE the first punch: "pads and bags, never people."\n3) The jab: straight out from the cheek, straight back to the cheek — one road, out and home. Ten quiet jabs in the air.\n4) The cross: the other hand\u2019s straight punch with a small hip turn — jab-jab-CROSS is the drumbeat.\n5) Play the pad rounds below and end every round with the rule, out loud: "pads and bags, never people."',
  say:'"Boxing is the pad-punching sport! Fists up by your cheeks — that is the guard. Jab — straight out, straight back, one road. Jab, jab, CROSS! But here is the rule, and it never changes: we punch PADS and BAGS, never people, never pets, never walls. Boxers are the fittest, politest punchers in the world — the rule is why."',
  notice:'On this card the word "boxing" leads at the top in big letters with the gym\u2019s scene below — the gloves and the training pads exactly as a pad-round looks.',
  ask:'"Where do the fists live in the guard? What road does the jab travel? What are the ONLY targets in boxing?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Guard up — fists by my cheeks!" "Jab, jab, CROSS!" or the rule, unprompted: "Pads and bags, never people!"',
  real:'Boxing gyms everywhere welcome kids\u2019 pad-work classes — and every one of them teaches the same rule first. On screen, watch one pad round: the trainer\u2019s pads are the only things the gloves ever touch. That is boxing\u2019s own discipline, visible.',
  play:'Pad Rounds: grown-up holds cushions as pads. Round one: thirty seconds of jab-jab-cross, feet bouncing, big finish cheer. Round two: jab on the MOVE — follow the pads around the room. Round three: child\u2019s choice, but the round must END with the rule said out loud. Three rounds, water break, guard-up parade lap. Fitness, straightness, and a rule kept with pride.',
  tips:'Hold the pads LOW for little arms — shoulder height is grown-up height. And if any punch ever drifts toward a person, the round pauses instantly and calmly: the rule is the lesson, and pausing proves it — warmly, every time.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the guard? What road does the jab take? What are the only targets?" Fists by the cheeks, out-and-back straight, pads and bags — three answers and the round is on.'},
 judo:{
  title:'The Judo Lesson',one:'The gentle way — grip soft, fall soft, and the mat is your oldest friend.',
  seoTitle:'Judo Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Jacket Grip, Balance and the Soft Break-Fall (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the judo jacket grip, the balance step and the starfish break-fall, with mat games from the gentle way itself.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches judo\u2019s two first skills — the gentle grip and the soft fall — because judo literally means "the gentle way," and its best gift to a four-year-old is knowing how to fall without hurt, for life.',
  what:'Judo is a mat art from Japan: partners in white suits grip each other\u2019s jackets, step, balance and unbalance — and the most practised skill of all is falling SAFELY, slapping the mat like a starfish.',
  mean:'Judo flips the fear: every beginner learns to FALL before learning to throw. The break-fall turns the scariest part of moving into a party trick — and it transfers to skating rinks, skateboards and slippery floors forever.',
  know:'Your child needs three judo ideas: the bow comes first — respect to the partner and the mat, every session; the grip is gentle — sleeve and elbow, holding a friend, not a rope; and the break-fall is the starfish — roll back, arms slap wide, soft and loud.',
  why:'"The gentle way" is the whole philosophy in two words: maximum effect with minimum effort, respect before everything. For four-year-olds, judo is manners, balance and safe falling in white pyjamas.',
  dev:'Safe falling (the break-fall is a lifelong injury-prevention skill), gentle-grip partner work, static and moving balance, and the respect ritual — bow first, always.',
  vocab:'judo, gentle, grip, balance, break-fall, mat, bow',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is judo — the gentle way on the mats."\n2) The bow: hands slide to the knees, small bow to the partner. Every judo session starts here — respect first, always.\n3) The grip: hold a partner\u2019s sleeve and elbow (or their jacket shoulders) — firm as holding a friend\u2019s hand, gentle as holding a bird.\n4) The break-fall: sit on a mat or bed, roll back one small roll, arms SLAP wide like a starfish — slap! Loud and soft at once. Three falls, giggling.\n5) Play the balance games below and close with the bow: "grip gentle, fall soft, bow first."',
  say:'"Judo means the gentle way! We bow first — to our partner and the mat. We grip the jacket soft — sleeve and elbow, like holding a friend. We step and balance like tall trees. And the best trick in judo is falling SOFTLY — roll back, arms out wide, SLAP the mat like a starfish. Everyone falls; judoka are the ones who fall well."',
  notice:'On this card the word "judo" stands big at the top with the mat\u2019s scene below — two partners in white suits gripping jackets exactly as a randori looks.',
  ask:'"What does judo mean?" "What comes first, every session?" "How do judoka fall — hard or soft like a starfish?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The gentle way!" "I gripped the sleeve soft!" or the proudest one: "SLAP — I break-fell like a starfish!"',
  real:'Judo lives in clubs across Japan, France, Brazil and every Olympic Games — and its break-fall lives in every swim school\u2019s safety roll and every skateboarder\u2019s first tumble. The gentle way is everywhere once you can see it.',
  play:'Starfish Fall School: on a bed or mat — sit, roll back, arms SLAP wide, giggle, repeat. Then the jacket-grip walk: partners grip sleeve-and-elbow and walk a slow circle, one leading, one following, roles swapped on a clap. Finish with tall-tree balance: one foot, arms wide, three counts — and the closing bow. Gentle, every step.',
  tips:'Make the fall the STAR, not the throw — four-year-olds who can break-fall beautifully are ready for everything else at their own pace. And never skip the bow: it costs three seconds and carries the whole philosophy.',
  check:'Ask: "What does judo mean? What do we do first, every session? How do we fall?" The gentle way, the bow, soft like a starfish — three answers and the mat is a friend.'},
 karate:{
  title:'The Karate Lesson',one:'Empty hand, strong block, straight punch — and manners counted in Japanese.',
  seoTitle:'Karate Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Blocks, the Straight Punch and the Karate Count (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the karate bow, the three blocks and the air-stop punch, with a counting kata for focus, manners and strong bodies.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches karate as the manners programme wearing a punch: the bow, the counted kata, the blocks before punches — and the control of stopping every punch in the air.',
  what:'Karate means "empty hand" — a Japanese art of blocks, punches and kicks done with no weapons: strong low blocks, straight punches that STOP in the air, and kata — patterns of moves counted out loud.',
  mean:'The hidden curriculum of karate is control: every punch stops short, every partner is bowed to, every class starts and ends with respect. Children learn that power and manners arrive together or not at all.',
  know:'Your child needs three karate ideas: the bow opens and closes everything; blocks come BEFORE punches — low, middle, high; and the punch is straight and STOPPED — air control proves the discipline.',
  why:'The counted kata — ichi, ni, san, shi — is focus training disguised as a drill: four moves in order, in Japanese, with a big voice. It is the wall\u2019s best first lesson in doing things IN ORDER.',
  dev:'Sequencing (the kata is an ordered plan), blocking mechanics, air-stopping control (punch power with a brake), and the counting itself — Japanese one-to-four is a genuine first foreign-language souvenir.',
  vocab:'karate, empty hand, block, punch, kata, bow, ichi ni san shi',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is karate — empty hand, strong blocks, straight punches."\n2) The bow: fists to the palm, small bow — oss! Every kata begins and ends here.\n3) The three blocks: LOW (arms cross down), MIDDLE (forearm across the tummy), HIGH (forearm over the head). Blocks before punches — say it, mean it.\n4) The stopped punch: straight out from the hip, STOP in the air — no partner, no target, full control. Control IS the skill.\n5) Teach the kata below and close with the bow: "block, block, block, punch — ichi ni san shi!"',
  say:'"Karate means empty hand — no weapons, just your body! We bow first: oss! Blocks come before punches: low, middle, high. Then the straight punch — out from the hip and STOP in the air, because karate power comes with karate control. And we count in Japanese: ichi, ni, san, shi! Bow. Oss!"',
  notice:'On this card the word "karate" is printed large at the top with the dojo\u2019s scene below — the child in the white suit mid-move exactly as a first kata looks.',
  ask:'"What does karate mean? What comes before punches? Where does the punch stop? What are the counting words?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Empty hand!" "Low, middle, high — blocks first!" or the counted kata: "Ichi, ni, san, SHI!"',
  real:'Karate lives in dojos on every street corner of the four countries this class calls home — the white suits and coloured belts are the world\u2019s most visible children\u2019s sport. Watch one kids\u2019 class: the bowing is not decoration, it is half the lesson.',
  play:'The First Kata: bow, then four moves down an imaginary line — low block (ichi), middle block (ni), high block (san), straight stopped punch (shi) — counted in a BIG voice. Turn, repeat the other way. Three lines make a whole kata: bow, and oss! Belt ceremony: any coloured ribbon tied on, one thing praised about the manners, not the punches.',
  tips:'The air-stop is the discipline engine — a punch that can stop can be trusted, and children feel that trust immediately. And count in Japanese every single time: four words of a second language, worn like a belt.',
  check:'Ask: "What does karate mean? What comes before punches? What does the punch do in the air? Count the kata." Empty hand, blocks, stops, ichi-ni-san-shi — four answers and the dojo is open.'},
 taekwondo:{
  title:'The Taekwondo Lesson',one:'The kicking art from Korea — fast feet, springy legs, and pads that go thwack.',
  seoTitle:'Taekwondo Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Front Kicks, Balance and the Pad Rule (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the taekwondo stance, the front kick and the roundhouse, with pad-kicking drills for balance, flexibility and loud counting.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches taekwondo, the kicking art of Korea: springy feet, one-leg balance, front kick and roundhouse at pads only — the wall\u2019s best one-leg balance training wearing a black belt.',
  what:'Taekwondo is Korea\u2019s martial art, famous for kicks: front kicks, side kicks and the spinning roundhouse, all drilled at pads and targets. Fast feet, flexible legs, and balance that holds on one leg while the other one works.',
  mean:'Kicking is the wall\u2019s finest one-leg balance challenge: the kicking leg swings while the standing leg owns the ground. Taekwondo trains that split — work and stand — a hundred times a class, which is why taekwondo kids hop like herons.',
  know:'Your child needs three taekwondo ideas: the stance is springy — feet apart, knees soft, hands up; the front kick is knee UP, foot OUT, foot BACK — a snap, not a stomp; and every kick lands on a PAD held by the grown-up — pads, never people, same rule as boxing\u2019s.',
  why:'One-leg balance is the quiet king of this whole class — every skater, batter and kicker borrows it — and taekwondo drills it with more joy per minute than anything else on the wall.',
  dev:'Single-leg dynamic balance, hip flexibility (the knee-up is a real stretch), kicking mechanics in snaps, and the pad-only discipline shared by every striking art.',
  vocab:'taekwondo, Korea, kick, front kick, roundhouse, pad, balance',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is taekwondo — the kicking art from Korea."\n2) The stance: feet apart, knees soft, fists up — springy like a kangaroo waiting for a joke.\n3) The front kick: knee UP high first, foot snaps OUT, foot comes BACK down — knee, out, back. Practise in slow motion five times, then fast.\n4) The roundhouse: same knee-up, but the foot swings ROUND sideways — a door swinging on the hip hinge. Slow-motion first, always.\n5) Play the pad-kick drill below and close: "knee up, snap out, pads only."',
  say:'"Taekwondo is the kicking sport from Korea! Springy stance, knees soft — then the front kick: knee UP, foot snaps OUT, foot comes BACK. Snap! And the roundhouse swings round like a door. Every kick lands on a PAD the grown-up holds — pads, never people. Ten kicks each leg, counting loud — and your standing leg learns to be a tree while the other one flies."',
  notice:'On this card the word "taekwondo" stands at the top in big letters with the dojang\u2019s scene below — the high kick toward the pad exactly as a kicking drill looks.',
  ask:'"Which country\u2019s art is taekwondo? What comes up first — the foot or the knee? What do kicks land on?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Knee up, snap out!" "My roundhouse went THWACK!" or the pad rule: "Kicks on pads, never people!"',
  real:'Taekwondo is an Olympic sport and a school-club staple across the US, Canada, Australia and NZ — the belts and the pad-thwacks are everywhere. Watch one kids\u2019 class: the heron-leg balance is the quiet skill behind every loud kick.',
  play:'Pad-Kick Drill: a cushion held at knee height by the grown-up — front kick, kick, kick, ten each leg, counted loud. Then the roundhouse cushion, swung gently sideways — five each side. Balance challenge to finish: standing-leg tree pose while the other foot "kicks the air" three slow times. Wobbles are the training; the pad is the target; the counting is the music.',
  tips:'Slow-motion kicks first — the knee-up order is the whole technique, and speed arrives on its own. And swap which leg kicks first: both legs deserve to learn, and balance grows twice as fast.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the kick start? What order — knee, out, back? What do kicks land on?" The knee, knee-out-back, pads — three answers and the dojang is springy.'},
 weightlifting:{
  title:'The Weightlifting Lesson',one:'Bend the knees, straight back, lift UP — the strong sport that is really a safe-lifting lesson for life.',
  seoTitle:'Weightlifting Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Safe Lift and the Knee-Bend Rule (Class 37)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the weightlifter\u2019s stance, the knees-bend back-straight lift and the slow lower, with soft-barbell games for strong, safe bodies.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches weightlifting\u2019s real gift: not the overhead press but the pattern underneath it — bend the KNEES, keep the back straight, hold it close — the safe-lifting rule every human body uses forever.',
  what:'Weightlifting is the Olympic strength sport: lifters pull a heavy barbell from the floor and press it clean overhead in one strong, precise moment — legs drive, back stays straight, the lift is speed and shape, not just muscle.',
  mean:'Under the chalk and the roar, weightlifting teaches the exact lifting pattern school bags and grocery bags will demand for a lifetime: knees bend, back straight, load close. Four is the perfect age to build the habit with a noodle.',
  know:'Your child needs three weightlifting ideas: the lift starts with BENT KNEES and a straight back — never a bent-back pull; the load stays CLOSE to the body; and everything comes DOWN slow — lifting is half the lift, lowering is the other half.',
  why:'The pool-noodle barbell makes the sport safe and silly, and the pattern it drills — knees, back, close, slow — is the single most reusable skill in this whole class: it walks out of the living room and into every heavy bag a child will ever carry.',
  dev:'The safe-lifting pattern (hip hinge with knee bend — a lifetime injury-prevention skill), overhead press with a braced middle, slow eccentric lowering, and lift-and-cheer effort cycles.',
  vocab:'weightlifting, barbell, lift, knees, straight back, press, strong',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is weightlifting — the strong sport of lifting things well."\n2) The setup: feet apart, noodle-barbell (or broom) in wide hands, knees BENT, back STRAIGHT, chest proud. That shape is the lift\u2019s foundation.\n3) The lift: push the floor away with the legs, stand tall, barbell stays CLOSE to the body — grazing the shirt buttons.\n4) The press: strong arms push it UP overhead — body tall, tummy braced — hold two counts, then lower it DOWN slow, slow, slow.\n5) Play the soft-barbell lift below and close: "knees bend, back straight, down slow."',
  say:'"Weightlifting is the strong sport! Feet apart, knees BENT, back straight as a plank — hold the barbell close to your buttons. Now push the floor away, stand tall, and PRESS it overhead — UP! Hold it like a trophy… and lower it DOWN slow, slow, slow. Strong lifters bend their knees, not their backs — and that is also how you lift your school bag forever."',
  notice:'On this card the word "weightlifting" stands big at the top with the platform\u2019s scene below — the barbell lifted strong overhead exactly as a clean-and-jerk looks.',
  ask:'"What bends — the knees or the back? Where does the barbell stay on the way up? What happens slow — the lift or the lower?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Knees bent, back straight!" "I pressed it UP like a trophy!" or the lifter\u2019s law: "Down slow — lowering is half the lift!"',
  real:'Weightlifting is the oldest Olympic strength sport — and its knee-bend rule lives in every school bag, grocery bag and pet-cat pickup in the world. Next heavy carry, do it out loud together: knees bend, back straight, hold it close.',
  play:'Soft-Barbell Club: pool noodle with two socks as plates. Five lifts with the pattern chanted every rep: "knees bend… back straight… hold close… UP… down slow." Then the grocery relay: carry soft items one at a time from the "store" (sofa) to the "kitchen" (chair) with perfect lifting form — the bag-lift pattern, practised as a game. Finish with a strong-lifter flex and a chalk-drawn medal.',
  tips:'Chant the pattern on every rep — the words carry the shape, and the shape becomes automatic. And never add real weight: the noodle IS the programme; the pattern is the prize that lasts a lifetime.',
  check:'Ask: "What bends, what stays straight? Where does the barbell travel? Which half is slow?" The knees, back straight, close to the body, the lower — four answers and the club is strong.'}
};
